// Auto-generated from the 2026 DSD Calendar in-service packages (SignIn_Quiz + Answer_Key docx).
// Pass threshold for this program: 80%.
const DSD_MODULES = [{"id":1,"key":"Dementia_1_Person_And_Disease","month":1,"title":"Dementia Series 1 of 5: Understanding the World of Dementia — The Person & the Disease","guidelines":["Dementia is a disease process, not normal aging. Occasional forgetfulness is normal; losing the ability to manage daily life is not.","Sudden or fluctuating confusion (hours to days) suggests delirium — a medical change of condition. Report to the licensed nurse immediately; the nurse assesses and notifies the provider.","Know each resident's story and baseline: preferences, routines, and abilities guide daily care.","Support remaining abilities — cue and set up rather than take over; never argue with or repeatedly correct a resident's reality.","Document behaviors factually and report what worked so the care plan can be updated."],"roles":{"Licensed Nurse":[{"q":"T / F — Dementia is a normal, expected part of aging.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — Alzheimer's disease is the most common type of dementia.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — If a resident with dementia is suddenly much more confused than usual, it is most likely just disease progression and does not require assessment.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — Residents with Lewy body dementia may be highly sensitive to antipsychotic medications.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — Person-centered care means adjusting facility routines to the resident's lifelong preferences whenever possible.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — In late-stage dementia, residents can no longer feel pain or emotion.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — Frontotemporal dementia often begins with personality, behavior, or language changes rather than memory loss.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"A resident with stable dementia develops fluctuating confusion and inattention over 48 hours. The MOST likely explanation is:","c":["Delirium from an acute medical cause that requires workup","Expected progression of the dementia","A new diagnosis of frontotemporal dementia","Normal age-related change"],"a":0},{"q":"Which action BEST reflects person-centered dementia care?","c":["Following the same morning routine for every resident on the hall","Learning that a resident was a farmer who woke at 5 AM, and honoring that wake-up time","Repeatedly reorienting a resident who asks for her deceased husband","Completing ADLs for the resident to save time"],"a":1},{"q":"Vascular dementia is most closely associated with:","c":["Strokes and small-vessel disease, often with stepwise decline","Visual hallucinations and antipsychotic sensitivity","Early personality change with younger onset","Vitamin C deficiency"],"a":0}],"CNA":[{"q":"T / F — Dementia is caused by disease in the brain — it is not a normal part of getting older.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — All residents with dementia have the same symptoms and decline in the same way.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — If a resident is suddenly much more confused today than yesterday, the CNA should report it to the nurse right away.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — Residents in late-stage dementia can no longer feel pain.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — Arguing with a resident or repeatedly correcting their memory usually helps them stay oriented.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — Knowing a resident's life story — their work, family, and routines — helps the CNA give better care.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — In early-stage dementia, many residents can still do most of their ADLs if given cues and extra time.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Resident A, who has dementia, is anxious and insists she must 'get home to my children.' What is the BEST response?","c":["Tell her that her children are grown and she lives in a nursing home now","Acknowledge her feelings ('You love your children — tell me about them') and then guide her toward an activity","Ignore her so the behavior is not reinforced","Tell her the doors are locked and she cannot leave"],"a":1},{"q":"While helping Resident B dress, you notice he can button his shirt when you hand him one item at a time. You should:","c":["Dress him completely yourself so care goes faster","Hand him one item at a time and cue each step so he does what he can","Leave him alone to dress and come back later","Ask the nurse to add total dressing assistance to the care plan"],"a":1}]}},{"id":2,"key":"Emergency_Disaster_Preparedness","month":1,"title":"Emergency and Disaster Preparedness / Life Safety","guidelines":["Know the location of the EOP on your unit and your assigned role before an emergency occurs.","Earthquake: drop, cover, and hold on during shaking; check residents and report status afterward. Do not self-initiate full evacuation except for immediate life threat.","Power failure: plug critical medical equipment into designated generator-powered (red) outlets and check equipment-dependent residents first.","Resident tracking is continuous during any evacuation or shelter-in-place event; report any unaccounted-for resident to the charge nurse immediately.","Participate in all scheduled drills and exercises; drills are documented as part of the facility training and testing program."],"roles":{"All Staff":[{"q":"T / F — Morning Star uses an all-hazards approach, meaning one flexible emergency framework that can be applied to any type of disaster.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — During an earthquake, staff should immediately begin evacuating all residents out of the building.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — Shelter-in-place means keeping residents inside the facility with doors and windows secured because outside conditions are more dangerous.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — Only nursing staff have assigned roles in an emergency.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — Every staff member should know where the Emergency Operations Plan (EOP) is located on their unit before an emergency happens.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — During an evacuation, resident tracking can wait until everyone has arrived at the assembly point.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"A wildfire smoke advisory is issued for Clovis. Air quality outside is poor. What should staff do FIRST?","c":["Open windows to keep air circulating","Close windows and exterior doors and follow direction regarding the HVAC system","Begin a full building evacuation","Move residents outside to the courtyard"],"a":1},{"q":"The power goes out at night and the emergency generator starts. Which action comes FIRST?","c":["Check residents who depend on electrical equipment (oxygen concentrators, feeding pumps) and confirm equipment is on red outlets","Call the utility company to ask when power will return","Reset the breaker panel yourself","Wait at the nursing station until the lights come back on"],"a":0}]}},{"id":3,"key":"Hand_Hygiene_PPE_Outbreaks","month":1,"title":"Hand Hygiene & PPE — Managing Outbreaks (Scabies, COVID, MRSA, GI/Norovirus)","guidelines":["Perform hand hygiene at the 5 moments: before touching a resident, before clean/aseptic procedures, after body fluid exposure risk, after touching a resident, and after touching resident surroundings.","Use soap and water — not ABHR — when hands are visibly soiled and during C. difficile or norovirus/GI illness care.","Don PPE in order (gown, mask, eye protection, gloves) and doff in order (gloves, eye protection, gown, mask), followed by hand hygiene. Remove gown and gloves before leaving the room.","Follow transmission-based precaution signage: contact (gown/gloves) for MRSA, scabies, C. diff, and GI illness; droplet/airborne per signage and facility protocol.","Report two or more similar illnesses in the same area (rash/itching, GI, respiratory) to the nurse and IP immediately — do not wait until end of shift.","Do not work while ill with fever, vomiting, or diarrhea; follow facility call-off and return-to-work policy."],"roles":{"Licensed Nurse":[{"q":"T / F — Alcohol-based hand rub (ABHR) is effective against C. difficile spores.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — Hand hygiene is required after removing gloves.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — Wearing gloves replaces the need for hand hygiene.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — During a suspected norovirus/GI outbreak, staff should use soap and water rather than ABHR.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — A line list tracks each outbreak case, including unit/room, symptom onset date, symptoms, and status.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — If a nurse suspects an outbreak cluster, it is acceptable to wait until the end of the shift to notify the IP.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — The correct doffing sequence begins with removing gloves.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Two residents on the same hall develop new vomiting and diarrhea within 24 hours. The nurse's BEST first action is to:","c":["Initiate contact precautions and notify the IP/DON immediately","Wait 24 hours to see if more residents become ill","Send both residents to group activities as usual","Only document the symptoms in the medical record"],"a":0},{"q":"A resident has confirmed scabies. Which precautions apply?","c":["Contact precautions — gown and gloves","Droplet precautions — surgical mask","Airborne precautions — N95 respirator","No precautions beyond standard"],"a":0},{"q":"Which is the correct DONNING order for full PPE?","c":["Gown, mask/respirator, eye protection, gloves","Gloves, gown, mask, eye protection","Mask, gloves, gown, eye protection","Eye protection, gloves, mask, gown"],"a":0}],"CNA":[{"q":"T / F — You should perform hand hygiene before touching a resident and again after touching them.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — If your gloves still look clean, it is okay to wear them from one resident's room into the next.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — When caring for a resident with C. diff, you should wash with soap and water instead of using hand sanitizer.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — When taking off PPE, gloves come off first.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — Hand sanitizer works well even when your hands are visibly dirty.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — New rashes, itching, vomiting, or diarrhea in a resident should be reported to the nurse right away.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — Only the Infection Preventionist is responsible for stopping the spread of infection in the facility.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"This week you have noticed three residents and one coworker scratching, with new rashes. You should:","c":["Apply lotion to the residents' skin and continue your assignment","Tell the nurse and IP right away — this could be a scabies cluster","Wait to see if the rashes go away on their own","Mention it at the staff meeting next month"],"a":1},{"q":"You are entering a contact-precautions room to provide full morning care. What PPE do you put on?","c":["Gloves only","Gown and gloves","N95 respirator only","No PPE if you will be quick"],"a":1}]}},{"id":4,"key":"Respiratory_Infection_Control_TB","month":1,"title":"Prevention and Control of Infection — Respiratory (COVID/Flu/RSV) & TB","guidelines":["Report new respiratory symptoms — or any 'not themselves' change in an elderly resident — to the licensed nurse the same shift. Atypical presentation (confusion, poor appetite, weakness) may be the first sign of infection.","Symptomatic residents are assessed, tested per protocol, and placed on precautions without waiting for results. A negative rapid test does not always rule out infection.","Influenza and pneumococcal vaccines must be offered with education; administration, refusal, or contraindication must be documented (F883).","TB screening is required at hire and annually for staff, and on admission for residents. Report cough >3 weeks, night sweats, weight loss, or coughing up blood to the IP.","Follow source-control masking triggers: symptomatic persons mask; follow facility-wide or unit masking direction during outbreaks.","Do not work while ill with fever or acute respiratory symptoms; follow the facility call-off and return-to-work policy."],"roles":{"Licensed Nurse":[{"q":"T / F — In elderly residents, new confusion or decreased appetite may be the first sign of a respiratory infection, even without fever.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — Under F883, the facility must offer influenza and pneumococcal vaccines, provide education, and document administration or refusal.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — A negative rapid test always rules out COVID-19 or influenza.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — TB warning signs include a cough lasting more than 3 weeks, night sweats, and unexplained weight loss.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — A resident with suspected active TB may remain in a shared room as long as they wear a surgical mask.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — A staff member with mild respiratory symptoms but no fever never needs to wear a mask.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — Respiratory surveillance includes tracking symptoms across units so clusters can be spotted early.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"A resident develops a new cough and a temperature of 100.9°F during flu season. The nurse's BEST response is to:","c":["Assess the resident, initiate precautions per protocol, notify the provider and IP, and test per protocol","Wait 24 hours to see if the fever resolves before doing anything","Test only, and isolate after a positive result comes back","Give acetaminophen and recheck at the next scheduled vital signs"],"a":0},{"q":"F883 requires which of the following to be documented for influenza vaccination?","c":["That education on benefits and side effects was provided","That the vaccine was administered, or refused, or medically contraindicated","The offer of the vaccine to the resident or representative","All of the above"],"a":3},{"q":"Which condition requires AIRBORNE precautions with a fitted respirator?","c":["Suspected active tuberculosis","Influenza","MRSA in a wound","Norovirus"],"a":0}],"CNA":[{"q":"T / F — A resident who suddenly seems 'not themselves' — more confused, eating less — could be starting an infection and should be reported to the nurse.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — You should report a resident's new cough or fever only if it lasts more than 3 days.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — If you have a fever and cough, it is okay to come to work as long as you wear a mask.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — Masking by people who have symptoms (source control) helps stop infectious droplets from spreading.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — Staff are screened for TB when hired and then annually per facility policy.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — Residents in isolation no longer need their call lights answered promptly.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — Covering coughs and performing hand hygiene help prevent the spread of respiratory infections.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"During flu season, Resident A has a new cough and feels warm. You take her temperature: 100.8°F. You should:","c":["Tell the licensed nurse right away, before moving on to other tasks","Give her water and check again tomorrow","Move her to the dining room so she is not alone","Write it in your notes and mention it at end of shift"],"a":0},{"q":"A resident is on droplet precautions for influenza. Before entering the room you should:","c":["Put on a surgical mask as directed by the door signage","Enter without PPE if you will only be a minute","Put on only gloves","Wait until the resident is asleep to enter"],"a":0}]}},{"id":8,"key":"Safe_Lifting_Transfers_ROM","month":2,"title":"General Lifting/Transferring Training & ROM (Give Yourself a Lift)","guidelines":["Check the resident's transfer status in the care plan/Kardex before every transfer; report mismatches between ability and care plan to the licensed nurse.","Mechanical lift transfers require two trained staff members and a pre-use sling inspection; damaged slings are removed from service immediately.","Use proper body mechanics: wide base, bend knees, load close, pivot — never twist; never lift a resident under the arms.","Perform ROM slowly to the point of resistance, never past pain; stop and report pain, stiffness, or swelling to the licensed nurse."],"roles":{"Licensed Nurse":[{"q":"You should check the resident's current transfer status in the care plan before every transfer, even for residents you know well.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"One trained staff member may operate a full-body mechanical lift alone if the resident is calm and cooperative.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"Twisting at the waist while supporting a resident's weight is acceptable if the transfer distance is short.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"Slings must be inspected for frayed straps, tears, and damaged loops before each use.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Passive ROM should be continued slightly past the point of pain to increase joint flexibility.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"A gait belt should be applied snugly over clothing, not against bare skin.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Raising the bed to working height before bedside care reduces back strain.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"A resident's care plan states “extensive assist x2 with mechanical lift.” The resident insists she can walk to the bathroom with one person helping. The nurse should:","c":["Honor the resident's request — resident choice always comes first","Follow the care plan, explain the reason, and refer the request to the IDT/therapy for re-assessment","Let the CNA use their judgment at the time of the transfer","Walk her with a gait belt as a one-time trial"],"a":1},{"q":"During passive ROM, the resident grimaces and the joint resists movement. The best action is to:","c":["Apply slightly more pressure to work through the resistance","Stop at the point of resistance and report the pain to the physician/document per policy","Continue the exercise but at a faster pace to finish sooner","Discontinue all ROM for this resident permanently"],"a":1},{"q":"Which of the following describes correct body mechanics for a transfer?","c":["Feet together, bend at the waist, arms extended","Feet together, knees locked, resident at arm's length","Wide base of support, bend at the knees, keep the resident close to your body","Wide base of support, twist at the waist to place the resident"],"a":2}],"CNA":[{"q":"If no gait belt is available, it is safe to transfer a resident by lifting under their arms (armpits).    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"When possible, transfer the resident toward their stronger side.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"If a mechanical lift sling looks slightly frayed, you may use it one last time and then report it.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"Wheelchair and bed brakes must be locked before every transfer.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"If a resident starts to fall during a transfer, ease them gently to the floor rather than trying to catch and hold their full weight.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"ROM exercises should be done quickly so the resident is not bothered for too long.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"Raising the bed to waist height and keeping the resident close to your body lowers your risk of back injury.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"The care plan says Resident A is a 2-person assist. Your coworker is busy and the resident urgently needs the bathroom. What should you do?","c":["Transfer the resident alone quickly — it's an emergency","Stay with the resident, reassure them, and call/use the call system to get a second staff member now","Ask the resident to try walking alone while you watch","Tell the resident they must wait until the next scheduled toileting round"],"a":1},{"q":"Halfway through a sit-to-stand lift transfer, the resident suddenly becomes dizzy and goes limp. What should you do?","c":["Keep going — finishing the transfer quickly is safest","Lower the resident safely to the nearest surface, stay with them, and call for the nurse","Leave the resident in the lift and go find the nurse","Unhook the sling immediately so the resident can sit down"],"a":1}]}},{"id":7,"key":"Problems_Needs_Of_The_Aged","month":2,"title":"Problems and Needs of the Aged, Chronically Ill, Acutely Ill and Disabled","guidelines":["Treat any new or sudden change (confusion, falls, appetite, continence, behavior) as a potential illness — report to the licensed nurse the same shift, never dismiss it as “just old age.”","Know the resident's baseline; in the elderly, small deviations (eating less, sleeping more, “not themselves”) are often the first sign of serious illness.","Watch for medication-related changes — dizziness, sedation, confusion, falls — especially after any medication change, and report promptly.","Preserve dignity: promote independence (do WITH, not FOR), offer choices, provide privacy, and speak to the resident — not over them."],"roles":{"Licensed Nurse":[{"q":"A sudden change in mental status in an elderly resident is usually just a normal part of aging.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"Elderly residents may have a serious infection without a significant fever.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Decreased thirst sensation in the elderly increases the risk of dehydration.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Depression is a normal, expected part of aging that does not require treatment.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"The more medications a resident takes, the higher the risk of interactions, falls, and confusion.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"A urinary tract infection in an elderly resident can present as new confusion rather than urinary symptoms.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"New urinary incontinence in a previously continent resident is an inevitable part of aging and does not warrant assessment.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"An 88-year-old resident is developing pneumonia. Which presentation is MOST likely?","c":["High fever with a loud productive cough, as in younger adults","New confusion, weakness, and decreased appetite with little or no fever","Severe chest pain as the only symptom","No observable change until the infection is advanced"],"a":1},{"q":"A resident started a new blood pressure medication and has fallen twice in three days. The nurse's best FIRST consideration is:","c":["The resident needs a restraint for safety","The falls are probably coincidence; continue monitoring only","The medication may be causing orthostatic hypotension or sedation — assess and notify the provider for medication review","The resident should be placed on bedrest until the falls stop"],"a":2},{"q":"Which of the following is a NORMAL change of aging?","c":["Sudden fluctuating confusion (delirium)","New urinary incontinence","Increased sensitivity to glare and slower adjustment to darkness","Loss of interest in all activities for weeks"],"a":2}],"CNA":[{"q":"If a resident who is normally alert becomes confused today, you should report it to the nurse right away.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Speaking as loudly as possible is the best way to help a hearing-impaired resident understand you.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"Older skin is thinner and tears more easily, so residents must be handled gently and never dragged during repositioning.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Residents should be offered fluids often even if they do not ask, because the sense of thirst decreases with age.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Doing everything for a resident is the kindest and best way to help them.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"Loneliness and loss of purpose can affect a resident's health as much as physical illness.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"It is okay to talk about a resident's condition with a coworker over the resident's head during care, as long as the resident seems confused.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"Resident A usually feeds herself and finishes most meals. For two days she has barely touched her food and seems weaker. What should you do?","c":["Nothing — appetites come and go in the elderly","Report it to the nurse — small changes like this can be the first sign of serious illness in an elderly resident","Just order her a sandwich instead of the regular meal","Wait one more day to see if the appetite returns"],"a":1},{"q":"You need to give instructions to a resident who is hard of hearing. What is the BEST approach?","c":["Stand behind her and speak as loudly as you can","Write everything down and skip talking","Face her, get her attention first, and speak clearly and slowly in a lower tone","Ask her roommate to relay the message"],"a":2}]}},{"id":5,"key":"Assessment_Pain_PASARR_Restraints","month":2,"title":"Resident Assessment: Vital Signs, Pain, PASARR, Behavior Interventions & Restraints","guidelines":["Know the resident's baseline; recheck vital signs with any abnormal reading, change of condition, new symptom, or post-fall, and report deviations to the licensed nurse/provider.","Assess pain by self-report and nonverbal cues (grimacing, guarding, restlessness); reassess after every intervention; CNAs report observed pain behaviors immediately.","Treat behavior as communication: assess for pain, toileting, hunger, fear, and overstimulation, and use nonpharmacologic interventions before any medication request.","Physical restraints require a medical symptom, least-restrictive trial, physician order, and informed consent — never discipline or convenience; side rails are used only per assessment and care plan; CNAs never apply or remove restraints on their own."],"roles":{"Licensed Nurse":[{"q":"Pain in a cognitively impaired resident can be assessed using nonverbal cues such as grimacing, guarding, and restlessness.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"If a resident verbally denies pain but grimaces and guards during care, the nurse should simply document “no pain reported.”    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"A physical restraint may be used temporarily for staff convenience when the unit is short-staffed.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"PASARR screening must be completed before admission to identify serious mental illness or intellectual disability.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Full side rails that prevent a resident from voluntarily getting out of bed may be a physical restraint.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Once a physician orders a restraint, it may remain in place indefinitely without re-evaluation.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"Vital signs should be rechecked and compared to the resident's baseline after a fall or any change of condition.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Before applying a physical restraint in a NON-emergency situation, which of the following is required?","c":["A documented medical symptom the restraint treats","A trial of less restrictive alternatives","A physician's order and informed consent","All of the above"],"a":3},{"q":"A resident with advanced dementia is restless, rubbing her knee, and strikes out when staff touch her leg during care. The BEST first action is to:","c":["Request a PRN antipsychotic for agitation","Assess for pain and other unmet needs before considering any medication","Apply a lap buddy so she cannot strike staff","Document the behavior and continue care as planned"],"a":1},{"q":"The primary purpose of PASARR is to:","c":["Determine the resident's Medicare payment category","Identify serious mental illness or intellectual disability and ensure needed specialized services","Screen for infectious diseases before admission","Establish the resident's code status"],"a":1}],"CNA":[{"q":"Behavior changes such as restlessness or striking out may be the resident's way of communicating pain or an unmet need.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"You should report a resident's grimacing or guarding during care to the nurse, even if the resident says nothing hurts.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"A lap tray or lap buddy that the resident cannot remove is never considered a restraint.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"If you see a restrained resident struggling, leaning badly, or caught in an awkward position, you should tell the nurse immediately.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Raising all four side rails is a good way to keep a confused resident safely in bed.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"A CNA may apply a restraint on their own if a resident is very agitated.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"Knowing a resident's normal vital signs and usual behavior helps you recognize when something is wrong.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Resident A is nonverbal with dementia. While you dress her, she moans and pulls her right arm away every time you touch it. What should you do?","c":["Continue dressing her quickly to get it over with","Stop, be gentle, and report it to the nurse — she may have pain or an injury in that arm","Dress her left arm only and skip the right sleeve","Note it to yourself and mention it at the end of the shift if there's time"],"a":1},{"q":"A resident keeps trying to climb out of his wheelchair. What should you do?","c":["Use a sheet to loosely tie him in so he can't fall","Report it to the nurse so the causes — toileting, pain, boredom, discomfort — can be assessed; never restrain the resident yourself","Park the wheelchair where staff can't see him so he calms down","Tell him firmly to stop climbing every time you walk by"],"a":1}]}},{"id":6,"key":"Mandated_Reporter_Abuse","month":2,"title":"Your Legal Duty: Reporting Elder & Dependent Adult Abuse — Mandated Reporter","guidelines":["Every employee, regardless of position, is a mandated reporter — the duty is personal and cannot be delegated or assumed to be covered by someone else.","Reasonable suspicion triggers reporting: notify the charge nurse/supervisor and Administrator immediately (same shift), followed by the verbal report and written SOC 341.","Serious bodily injury allegations: reported to CDPH and law enforcement within 2 hours (F609); all other allegations within 24 hours.","Report first — never investigate, confront the accused, or wait for proof; the facility separates the alleged perpetrator from residents and conducts the investigation (F610)."],"roles":{"Licensed Nurse":[{"q":"Only licensed staff are mandated reporters; CNAs, dietary, and housekeeping staff are not.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"If an abuse allegation involves serious bodily injury, the report to CDPH and law enforcement must be made within 2 hours.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"You should first investigate and confirm that abuse actually occurred before making a report.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"Reasonable suspicion of abuse is enough to trigger your duty to report.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"A staff member who reports suspected abuse in good faith is legally protected from retaliation.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Borrowing money from a resident is acceptable as long as the resident agrees and you pay it back.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"A resident-to-resident altercation that causes injury must be reported and investigated like any other abuse allegation.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"A CNA tells you a resident said a night-shift staff member handled her roughly, and you observe a small bruise on the resident's wrist. Your FIRST actions should be to:","c":["Wait and observe the resident over the next few shifts for more evidence","Ensure the resident's safety, and immediately notify the Administrator/Abuse Coordinator to begin the reporting process","Call the accused staff member at home to hear their side first","Document the bruise in the chart and take no further action unless it worsens"],"a":1},{"q":"Which form is used in California for the written report of suspected elder/dependent adult abuse?","c":["CMS-2567","SOC 341","MDS 3.0","SBAR communication form"],"a":1},{"q":"Which of the following is an example of misappropriation under F602?","c":["Helping a resident count her own money at her request, with another staff member present","Delivering a resident's mail unopened","Using a resident's debit card to buy yourself lunch, intending to pay it back","Locking a resident's valuables in the facility safe per policy"],"a":2}],"CNA":[{"q":"Every employee in the facility, no matter their job, is a mandated reporter of suspected abuse.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"If you are not 100% sure abuse happened, you should keep it to yourself until you have proof.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"Yelling at, teasing, or humiliating a resident can be abuse even if you never touch them.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Repeatedly ignoring a resident's call light can be a form of neglect.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"If the person you suspect is your supervisor, there is no one else you can report to.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"You can get in legal trouble for making a good-faith abuse report that turns out to be unfounded.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"Unexplained or patterned bruising on a resident should be reported to the nurse right away.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"You see a coworker yank Resident A roughly by the arm and yell at her to hurry up. What should you do?","c":["Mind your own business — it's between them","Report it immediately to the charge nurse/supervisor — you have a personal duty to report what you saw","Wait to see if it happens again before saying anything","Talk it over with the other CNAs to see if they agree it was abuse"],"a":1},{"q":"A resident's daughter tells you money has gone missing from her mother's purse twice this month. What should you do?","c":["Suggest the family keep better track of the money","Report it to the nurse and supervisor right away — possible financial abuse must be reported like any other abuse","Quietly ask other staff if they took it","Tell the daughter to file a police report herself and leave it at that"],"a":1}]}},{"id":9,"key":"Dementia_2_What_Is_Abuse","month":3,"title":"Dementia Series 2 of 5: What is Abuse?","guidelines":["Abuse includes verbal, mental, physical, and sexual abuse and neglect; it does not require visible injury or the resident's ability to remember or report the event.","Rushing care, purposeful ignoring, and infantilizing (baby talk, pet names, treating adults as children) are at-risk practices that erode dignity and can constitute abuse or neglect.","Staff who feel frustration during care must pause, step away safely, or hand off to a coworker — and offer the same relief to coworkers who are struggling.","Suspected abuse is reported immediately to the charge nurse, DON, or Administrator — never delayed to gather proof, and never investigated by the observer alone."],"roles":{"Licensed Nurse":[{"q":"Abuse must leave a visible injury to be considered abuse under F600.     TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"A resident with advanced dementia who cannot remember or report mistreatment can still be a victim of abuse.     TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Speaking to a resident in baby talk is a harmless way to show affection.     TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"Repeatedly forcing care over a dementia resident's active resistance, rather than stopping and re-approaching, can constitute abuse or neglect.     TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"If you witness a coworker yelling at a resident, you should report it even if the resident does not seem upset.     TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Staff frustration is a normal human response; the professional duty is to recognize it and safely step away or ask for relief.     TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Suspected abuse should be reported only after you have gathered enough proof.     TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"Which of the following is an example of mental/verbal abuse?","c":["Explaining each step of care before performing it","Threatening to withhold dessert if the resident does not cooperate with care","Offering the resident a choice between two outfits","Documenting a resident's resistive behavior objectively"],"a":1},{"q":"A resident with dementia resists showering and begins hitting at staff. The BEST response is:","c":["Have two staff hold her arms so the shower can be finished quickly","Stop, step back, and try again later with a calm person-centered approach; report the resistance to the nurse and look for an unmet need","Skip her showers going forward without telling anyone","Tell her she will lose activity privileges unless she cooperates"],"a":1},{"q":"Under F600, the definition of abuse includes:","c":["Only physical acts that cause injury","Willful infliction of injury, unreasonable confinement, intimidation, or punishment resulting in physical harm, pain, or mental anguish","Only acts committed with a documented intent to injure the resident","Only events the resident personally reports"],"a":1}],"CNA":[{"q":"Ignoring a resident's call light on purpose can be a form of neglect.     TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Calling residents “sweetie” or “baby” and using baby talk shows respect.     TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"Because residents with dementia forget quickly, rough or hurried care does not really harm them.     TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"If you feel frustrated during care, it is okay to pause and ask a coworker to take over.     TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Reporting suspected abuse is only the charge nurse's job.     TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"Behaviors like hitting or yelling are often the resident's way of communicating an unmet need.     TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"You should report suspected abuse immediately to the charge nurse, DON, or Administrator.     TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"You see a coworker jerk Resident A's arm and snap, “Hurry up — we don't have all day.” Resident A laughs it off. What should you do?","c":["Nothing — the resident wasn't bothered by it","Report what you saw immediately to the charge nurse or supervisor","Wait and see if the coworker does it again before saying anything","Handle it privately by talking to the coworker and leaving it there"],"a":1},{"q":"While washing Resident A's hand, she clenches her fist, pulls away, and cries out. What should you do?","c":["Force her fingers open quickly so you can finish","Stop, speak calmly, try a different approach or come back later, and report the resistance to the nurse","Skip washing her hand and not mention it to anyone","Raise your voice firmly so she understands she must cooperate"],"a":1}]}},{"id":10,"key":"Elopement_Prevention","month":3,"title":"Elopement: Prevention and Action","guidelines":["Elopement risk is assessed on admission, with change of condition, and after any exit-seeking behavior; interventions are individualized and care-planned.","Door alarms, delayed-egress doors, and wander-management devices are checked and documented every shift; malfunctions are reported to Maintenance immediately and never bypassed.","Any staff member who cannot locate a resident immediately notifies the charge nurse and initiates the missing-resident procedure and head count; if the resident is not found promptly on premises, 911 is called and the Administrator/DON, physician, and responsible party are notified.","Every elopement or near-elopement generates an incident report, a same-shift huddle, a root cause review, and a care plan update; drills are conducted on all shifts."],"roles":{"Licensed Nurse":[{"q":"Elopement risk must be assessed on admission and again with any significant change in condition.     TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"A resident who wanders inside the facility but has not tried to exit has, by definition, eloped.     TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"Residents are at highest risk of elopement during the first days after admission.     TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"If a door alarm keeps sounding falsely, staff may silence or disable it until Maintenance arrives.     TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"A cognitively intact resident who leaves the facility after signing out with staff knowledge has eloped.     TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"Statements like “I need to go home to feed my dog” should be reported and addressed in the care plan.     TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"The care plan must be updated after any elopement or near-elopement event.     TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"A resident on the wander-risk program cannot be located during rounds. The nurse's FIRST action should be:","c":["Begin documenting the circumstances in the clinical record","Notify the charge nurse and initiate the missing-resident procedure and head count immediately","Wait 15 minutes to see if the resident returns from another unit","Call the resident's family to ask if they picked the resident up"],"a":1},{"q":"Who is responsible for making the required elopement report to CDPH?","c":["The CNA who discovered the resident missing","The resident's responsible party","The Administrator/DON (facility leadership) per facility policy","Local law enforcement after the 911 call"],"a":2},{"q":"Which combination is MOST protective against elopement?","c":["PRN sedation for any resident who paces near doors","Keeping all at-risk residents in their rooms with doors closed","Individualized engagement, functioning egress alarms and device checks, and increased monitoring","A one-time orientation reminder that residents may not leave"],"a":2}],"CNA":[{"q":"Every staff member, in every department, shares responsibility for elopement prevention.     TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"If you see an unfamiliar person walking out the door with a resident, you should assume they are family and continue your work.     TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"Sundowning in the late afternoon and evening can increase a resident's exit-seeking behavior.     TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"When a door alarm sounds, you should respond and check immediately, even if you believe it is false.     TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"A resident asking to “go home” repeatedly is nothing to report because it is common in dementia.     TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"It is safe to let a resident follow you through a keypad-secured door as long as they seem calm.     TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"During a missing-resident search, you should search your assigned zone and report back even if you find nothing.     TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"You find the courtyard gate propped open with a cone. What should you do FIRST?","c":["Leave it — Maintenance must have a reason for it","Close and secure the gate, then immediately report it to the charge nurse and Maintenance","Watch the gate for a while to see if anyone comes back for the cone","Mention it at the end of your shift"],"a":1},{"q":"Resident A, who has dementia, is standing at the exit door saying, “My shift is over — I'm going home now.” What is the BEST response?","c":["Tell her firmly that she lives here now and walk away","Approach calmly, walk with her, redirect her to an activity or snack, and notify the nurse of the exit-seeking","Block the door with your body and tell her she is not allowed to leave","Ignore it — she says this every day and never gets out"],"a":1}]}},{"id":11,"key":"Resident_Dignity_Privacy","month":3,"title":"Preservation of Resident Dignity, Including Provision for Privacy","guidelines":["Knock, announce, and wait before entering any resident room; close the door and pull the curtain fully before care; expose only the area being cared for.","Use adult, respectful language and preferred names; cue toileting and personal needs discreetly; never discuss residents in public areas of the building.","Dining is dignified: clothing protectors are offered (not imposed), assistance is provided seated at eye level and unhurried, and labels like “feeder” are never used.","No resident photos on personal devices and no social media posts about residents, ever; suspected violations are reported to the DON or Administrator immediately."],"roles":{"Licensed Nurse":[{"q":"Pulling the privacy curtain most of the way closed is sufficient during incontinence care.     TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"Discussing a resident's condition in the hallway is acceptable as long as you do not use the resident's name.     TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"Staff should knock, announce themselves, and wait for a response before entering a resident's room.     TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Residents have the right to private phone calls and private space for visitors.     TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Using terms like “feeders” or “the hip in Room 4” in report is acceptable clinical shorthand.     TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"During a treatment or dressing change, only staff needed for the procedure should be present.     TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Staff may open a resident's mail for them only with the resident's permission.     TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"During a dressing change, a housekeeper opens the door to grab supplies. The BEST action is:","c":["Continue — the housekeeper is facility staff","Cover the resident, ask the housekeeper to return later, and ensure the door and curtain are secured","Pause and ask the housekeeper to observe for training purposes","Nothing — dressing changes are not private procedures"],"a":1},{"q":"Which is the dignified way to prompt a resident who needs toileting reminders while she is in the dining room?","c":["Ask her across the dining room if she needs the bathroom","Approach quietly, speak at eye level, and offer assistance discreetly","Place a reminder sign on the back of her chair","Take her wheelchair to the bathroom without asking"],"a":1},{"q":"Which statement about photographs of residents is correct?","c":["Photos are acceptable if the resident's face is not visible","Photos on a personal phone are acceptable if they are never posted","Residents are never photographed on personal devices; facility photos require proper consent per policy","Photos may be posted to social media with the resident's verbal consent"],"a":2}],"CNA":[{"q":"Knocking and waiting before entering shows respect and protects privacy.     TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"When two staff give care together, it is fine to chat with each other over the resident about weekend plans.     TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"During a bed bath, you should cover the resident and expose only the area you are washing.     TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Clothing protectors must be used on every resident at every meal.     TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"Posting a funny video of a resident on social media is okay if the resident smiled in it.     TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"Calling residents by their preferred name supports dignity.     TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"You should close the door AND pull the privacy curtain during personal care.     TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"You are helping Resident A dress when another staff member opens the door to grab supplies while the resident is uncovered. What should you do?","c":["Nothing — it only took a few seconds","Cover the resident immediately and ask the staff member to wait or come back later","Finish dressing the resident first, then mention it","Move the resident to face away from the door"],"a":1},{"q":"In the dining room, Resident A spills food down his shirt. The dignified response is to:","c":["Announce that he needs a bib so someone brings one","Quietly offer a clothing protector and help him change discreetly after the meal","Take his plate away so he cannot spill more","Ask the residents at his table to keep an eye on him"],"a":1}]}},{"id":12,"key":"Resident_Rights_Civil_Rights","month":3,"title":"Resident Rights and Civil Rights","guidelines":["Residents retain all civil and legal rights on admission, including self-determination in schedules, care, visitors, and daily routines.","A refusal of care or treatment is respected, reported to the licensed nurse, explored, and re-offered — never punished or ignored.","Residents are presumed to make their own decisions; conservators/guardians act only within their legal scope, and residents remain involved to the fullest extent possible.","Grievances are routed promptly per the grievance process and no form of retaliation is tolerated."],"roles":{"All Staff":[{"q":"Residents keep their civil and legal rights when they are admitted to a nursing facility.     TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"A resident has the right to refuse a medication, a shower, or a therapy session.     TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"If a resident has a conservator or guardian, staff may disregard the resident's own preferences.     TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"Filing a grievance can result in a resident losing privileges.     TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"Residents have the right to receive visitors of their choosing.     TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Only nursing staff need to know about resident rights; other departments are exempt.     TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"A resident refuses her morning shower. The BEST response is:","c":["Tell her the schedule requires it and shower her anyway","Respect the refusal, inform the nurse, explore her preference, and offer again later","Document her as noncompliant and stop offering showers","Ask her family to pressure her into showering"],"a":1},{"q":"Which of the following is a violation of resident rights?","c":["Knocking and waiting before entering a room","Opening and reading a resident's mail without permission","Offering a choice between two meal options","Helping a resident put a grievance in writing"],"a":1}]}},{"id":13,"key":"Communication_Elder_Population","month":4,"title":"Communication Skills for the Elder Population","guidelines":["Face the resident at eye level, lower your pitch (never shout), reduce background noise, and make sure glasses and hearing aids are on and working.","Speak to residents as adults: preferred name, normal tone, short sentences, one instruction at a time — no pet names or baby talk.","With dementia, respond to the feeling (validation) rather than arguing facts; approach from the front and give time to respond.","With aphasia or dysarthria: allow extra time, use yes/no questions and communication aids, and never pretend to understand.","Confirm understanding with teach-back, and report any NEW speech or comprehension change to the nurse right away — it may signal a stroke or delirium."],"roles":{"Licensed Nurse":[{"q":"Shouting is the best way to communicate with a resident who is hard of hearing.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"A sudden new onset of slurred speech should be treated as a possible acute change in condition and assessed immediately.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"With receptive aphasia, a resident may hear your words clearly but be unable to understand their meaning.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Reality orientation (correcting the resident with the facts) is the preferred approach for a resident with advanced dementia who is asking for her deceased mother.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"Teach-back means asking the resident to repeat instructions back in their own words.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Elderspeak such as pet names and a sing-song tone is acceptable if the staff member intends it affectionately.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"Approaching from the front and positioning yourself at eye level improves communication with cognitively impaired residents.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"A resident with expressive aphasia after a stroke is struggling to answer your assessment questions. The BEST strategy is to:","c":["Finish the resident's sentences to save time","Ask yes/no questions and allow extra time to respond","Speak louder and repeat the same open-ended question","Defer all communication to the family"],"a":1},{"q":"Which is the BEST example of teach-back after educating a resident about a new anticoagulant?","c":["\"Do you understand everything I explained?\"","\"Any questions before I go?\"","\"Tell me in your own words how you will take this medication and what you'll watch for.\"","Handing the resident the medication information leaflet"],"a":2},{"q":"A resident with moderate dementia insists she must leave to pick up her children from school. The BEST response is:","c":["\"Your children are grown — you live in a nursing home now.\"","\"Tell me about your children,\" then guide her toward a comforting activity","Ignore the statement and continue your task","Remind her every time she asks that the doors are locked"],"a":1}],"CNA":[{"q":"Making sure a resident's glasses and hearing aids are on before care helps communication and prevents sensory deprivation.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"It is okay to call residents \"sweetie\" or \"honey\" as long as you are being kind.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"You should approach a resident with dementia from behind and begin care quickly so it is over sooner.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"Turning off the TV and reducing background noise makes it easier for a hard-of-hearing resident to understand you.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"If a resident with aphasia is struggling to find a word, you should hurry them along so they don't get frustrated.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"When helping a confused resident, you should give one-step instructions.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"New trouble speaking or understanding words should be reported to the nurse right away.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"You explain a shower to a resident who is hard of hearing, and she looks confused. What should you do NEXT?","c":["Shout the same words louder","Face her at eye level, lower your pitch, and repeat slowly and clearly with the TV off","Skip the explanation and start the shower — she will figure it out","Speak faster so she only has to concentrate for a moment"],"a":1},{"q":"A resident with dementia tells you she is waiting for the bus to go to work. The BEST response is:","c":["\"You haven't worked in thirty years.\"","Keep correcting her until she accepts where she is","Respond to the feeling — chat about the job she loved — then invite her to an activity","Walk away and let her wait by the door"],"a":2}]}},{"id":14,"key":"HIPAA_Confidentiality","month":11,"title":"Confidentiality of Resident Information (HIPAA)","guidelines":["PHI is any health information that could identify a resident — a name is not required. Room number + diagnosis, a photo, or a description that someone could recognize is PHI.","Access only the records of residents you are assigned to care for and only what your job requires (minimum necessary). Curiosity look-ups are violations even if nothing is shared.","No resident conversations in public areas; direct family and caller questions to the licensed nurse, who verifies identity and authorization; never confirm or deny that someone is a resident without authorization.","Never photograph or record residents or post anything about them on social media — not even in the background. Do not text PHI on personal devices, share passwords, or leave a screen logged in.","Report any suspected breach — including your own mistake — to your supervisor and the Administrator (Privacy Officer) immediately. California requires the facility to report a breach to CDPH and the resident within 15 business days of detection, so every hour counts. No retaliation for good-faith reports."],"roles":{"All Staff":[{"q":"T / F — A resident's room number combined with their diagnosis can be PHI even if their name is never used.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — It is okay to look up the record of a resident you are not assigned to if you are genuinely concerned about them.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — Posting a photo from a facility activity on your personal social media is acceptable if residents are only in the background.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — The minimum necessary rule means you should access only the information needed to do your job.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — If you accidentally send resident information to the wrong person, you should report it to your supervisor immediately.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — Discussing a resident's care with the oncoming shift during handoff is a HIPAA violation.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — If you think you may have caused a privacy breach, you should wait until you are sure before reporting it, because the facility has plenty of time to investigate.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"You are a housekeeper. A family member stops you in the hall and asks how their father is doing today. Your BEST response is to:","c":["Share what you noticed while cleaning his room","Politely direct them to the licensed nurse or designated staff for an update","Tell them he seems fine so they don't worry","Confirm his room number so they can find him and walk away"],"a":1},{"q":"Which of the following is a HIPAA breach?","c":["A nurse giving change-of-shift report to the oncoming nurse","A CNA telling a roommate about a well-known local resident's admission","A nurse reporting a change in condition to the physician","A CNA documenting ADL care in the resident's record"],"a":1},{"q":"A coworker shows you a video on their phone of a resident who is confused, laughing about it. What should you do?","c":["Laugh along and ask them not to post it","Tell them to delete it and say nothing further","Report it immediately to your supervisor and the Administrator so it can be investigated as a privacy violation and possible abuse","Ask them to send you a copy as proof"],"a":2}]}},{"id":15,"key":"Interpersonal_Relationships_Communication","month":4,"title":"Interpersonal Relationships and Communication Skills","guidelines":["Maintain professional boundaries: no gifts, loans, personal business, or social media connections with residents or families.","Use closed-loop communication in handoffs — repeat back what you heard and confirm before acting.","Resolve conflict directly, privately, and respectfully; never in front of residents or families. Escalate through the chain of command when unresolved.","With upset families: listen fully, acknowledge the concern, avoid defensiveness, and follow through or connect them to the right person. Offer the grievance process."],"roles":{"All Staff":[{"q":"Disagreements between coworkers should be discussed privately — never in front of residents or families.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Accepting a small cash gift from a grateful family is acceptable as long as it is under $20.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"Closed-loop communication means repeating back what you heard to confirm the message.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"If you cannot resolve a conflict with a coworker directly, the next step is to bring it to your supervisor.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Helping another department is not your responsibility if the task is not in your job description.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"When a family member is upset, the best first response is to explain why they are wrong.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"During handoff you receive instructions that are unclear. Your BEST action is to:","c":["Make your best guess and carry on","Ask clarifying questions and repeat back what you heard until you both agree","Plan to ask someone else about it later in the shift","Skip the task since the instructions weren't clear"],"a":1},{"q":"A family member angrily says her mother's laundry keeps going missing. Which response BEST reflects HEART values?","c":["Immediately defend the facility and explain that laundry is rarely lost","Listen fully, acknowledge her frustration, and follow through or connect her with the right person — offering the grievance process if she wishes","Tell her to file a grievance and walk away","Promise the laundry will never be lost again"],"a":1}]}},{"id":16,"key":"Workplace_Violence_Prevention","month":4,"title":"Violence in the Workplace","guidelines":["Know the four types of workplace violence; Type 2 (resident/visitor toward staff) is the most common in health care.","Watch for escalation warning signs and use de-escalation: calm low voice, give space, listen, offer choices, call for help early.","If a resident becomes aggressive during care: step back, protect yourself without retaliating, call for help, and report to the charge nurse for assessment.","Report ALL incidents including verbal threats and near-misses the same shift. Incidents are recorded in the violent incident log required by Cal/OSHA §3342."],"roles":{"All Staff":[{"q":"Type 2 workplace violence — aggression from a resident or visitor toward staff — is the most common type in health care.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Verbal threats that do not involve physical contact do not need to be reported.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"If a resident with dementia strikes you during care, it must still be reported and recorded even though the resident did not intend harm.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"California requires the facility to keep a log of violent incidents.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"An employee can be disciplined for reporting a workplace violence concern if the report turns out to be unfounded.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"The best first response to an escalating, agitated person is to stand close and speak firmly and loudly.    TRUE / FALSE","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"A visitor in the hallway is yelling, pacing, and clenching his fists. What is your BEST first action?","c":["Physically block him from moving farther down the hall","Give him space, use a calm low voice, and call or send for help","Ignore him and continue your task — he will calm down on his own","Firmly tell him he is wrong and must leave immediately"],"a":1},{"q":"Under the facility Workplace Violence Prevention Plan, which incidents must be reported?","c":["Only incidents that cause a physical injury","Only incidents involving a weapon or the police","All incidents, including verbal threats and near-misses","Only incidents between two employees"],"a":2}]}},{"id":17,"key":"Dementia_3_Listening_Speaking","month":5,"title":"Dementia Series 3 of 5: Listening and Speaking (F947 / F941)","guidelines":["Approach residents with dementia from the front, at eye level, using their preferred name and a calm adult tone — never elderspeak.","Give instructions one step at a time, paired with gestures; allow time to respond before repeating or rephrasing.","Use validation and gentle redirection; do not argue with, quiz, or forcibly reorient a distressed resident.","Report nonverbal pain cues and any sudden change in communication or alertness to the licensed nurse immediately."],"roles":{"Licensed Nurse":[{"q":"In early-stage dementia, residents may have word-finding difficulty but can still follow most conversations.    ( True / False )","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"The ability to understand tone of voice and facial expression is usually retained longer than the ability to understand words.    ( True / False )","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Repeatedly correcting a resident's mistaken beliefs (for example, that a deceased parent is alive) is the recommended way to reorient them.    ( True / False )","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"Raising your voice is an effective way to help a resident with dementia understand you.    ( True / False )","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"Multi-step directions should be broken into one step at a time.    ( True / False )","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Elderspeak (baby talk) is comforting and appropriate for residents with dementia.    ( True / False )","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"In late-stage dementia, behavior may be the resident's primary way of communicating needs.    ( True / False )","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Resident A, who has dementia, is agitated and insists she must go home to feed her children. The BEST response is:","c":["“Your children are grown — you live here now.”","“You can't leave; the doors are locked.”","“You love your children. Tell me about them,” then redirect to an activity","Ignore the statement until she stops repeating it"],"a":2},{"q":"The most effective way to give instructions during morning care is:","c":["Explain all the steps before starting so there are no surprises","One short step at a time, paired with gestures or demonstration","Written instructions the resident can refer to","Speak loudly and repeat quickly until the resident complies"],"a":1},{"q":"A resident who normally chats during care suddenly stops speaking and appears drowsy. The nurse should:","c":["Chart it as expected dementia progression","Try again tomorrow","Assess the resident and evaluate for an acute change of condition, then notify the provider as indicated","Ask the CNA to document the resident is “uncooperative”"],"a":2}],"CNA":[{"q":"You should approach a resident with dementia from the front and get down to eye level before speaking.    ( True / False )","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Arguing with a resident about what is real usually increases their distress.    ( True / False )","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"If a resident does not respond right away, immediately repeat the question louder.    ( True / False )","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"Giving two or three instructions at once helps residents with dementia finish care faster.    ( True / False )","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"A smile and a calm tone can communicate safety even when the resident no longer understands the words.    ( True / False )","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"If a resident pauses to find a word, it is helpful to finish every sentence for them and move on.    ( True / False )","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"Grimacing, guarding, or crying out during care may be signs of pain that should be reported to the nurse.    ( True / False )","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Resident B says, “I have to catch the bus to work.” The BEST response is:","c":["“You haven't worked in twenty years.”","“You were always a hard worker! Tell me about your job,” then invite him to an activity","“There is no bus. Sit down.”","Walk away so you don't encourage the idea"],"a":1},{"q":"Resident C becomes upset and pushes your hand away when you start her shower. What should you do?","c":["Continue quickly so it's over sooner","Tell her she has no choice because showers are scheduled","Stop, step back, speak calmly, try a different approach or return later, and tell the nurse","Have two more staff hold her still"],"a":2}]}},{"id":18,"key":"Fall_Prevention_Intervention","month":5,"title":"Fall Prevention & Intervention (F689; Jericho Falls Prevention Policy & Procedure, eff. 01/15/2026)","guidelines":["Universal fall precautions apply to every resident on every shift: call light in reach, bed low and locked, non-skid footwear, clear dry pathways, prompt call light response.","Purposeful rounding addresses pain, positioning, personal/toileting needs, and placement of belongings; at-risk residents receive proactive toileting.","After a fall, the resident is NOT moved until the licensed nurse completes the injury assessment; CNAs stay with the resident and call for the nurse.","Neuro checks are performed and documented for unwitnessed falls and known/suspected head strikes per the Jericho Falls Prevention Policy & Procedure.","Every fall is followed by a huddle, root cause review, and individualized care plan update communicated to all shifts."],"roles":{"Licensed Nurse":[{"q":"An unwitnessed fall is treated as a possible head strike and requires neurological checks per facility policy.    ( True / False )","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"A resident found on the floor should be helped up quickly to preserve their dignity, then assessed once back in bed.    ( True / False )","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"Intrinsic fall risk factors include muscle weakness, orthostatic hypotension, and medication side effects.    ( True / False )","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"The main purpose of the post-fall huddle is to identify which staff member was at fault.    ( True / False )","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"After a fall, the care plan must be updated with individualized interventions per facility policy.    ( True / False )","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Purposeful rounding addresses pain, positioning, personal (toileting) needs, and placement of items within reach.    ( True / False )","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"If a resident shows no visible injuries immediately after a fall, no further monitoring is required.    ( True / False )","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"You are called to a resident found on the floor beside the bed. Your FIRST action is to:","c":["Assist the resident back to bed with two staff","Assess the resident for injury before anyone moves them","Complete the incident report","Call the responsible party"],"a":1},{"q":"Which of the following is an EXTRINSIC fall risk factor?","c":["Orthostatic hypotension","Poor lighting and a cluttered pathway","Cognitive impairment","Lower-extremity weakness"],"a":1},{"q":"Neurological checks are required after:","c":["Every fall, witnessed or not, even with no head involvement","Witnessed falls only","An unwitnessed fall or any known/suspected head strike","Only when the provider specifically orders them"],"a":2}],"CNA":[{"q":"You should answer call lights promptly, even for residents who are not assigned to you.    ( True / False )","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"If you find a resident on the floor, help them up right away and then tell the nurse.    ( True / False )","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"Non-skid footwear and clear, dry pathways are universal fall precautions.    ( True / False )","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Offering toileting during rounds can prevent falls.    ( True / False )","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"When care is finished, the bed should be left in the lowest position with the brakes locked.    ( True / False )","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Fall interventions are the same for every resident in the facility.    ( True / False )","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"What the CNA saw before and after a fall is not needed at the post-fall huddle.    ( True / False )","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"You walk into a room and find Resident A sitting on the floor by the bathroom door. What do you do FIRST?","c":["Help her stand up before anyone sees","Stay with her, do not move her, and call for the licensed nurse","Go find the DON to report it","Bring her wheelchair over and lift her into it"],"a":1},{"q":"Resident B fell last week and keeps trying to stand up from his wheelchair without help. The BEST response is to:","c":["Tell him to stop standing up","Leave him alone — he has the right to fall","Report the behavior to the nurse, keep him where staff can see him, and offer toileting and activity per his care plan","Use a lap belt to keep him in the chair"],"a":2}]}},{"id":19,"key":"Food_Nutritional_Services","month":5,"title":"Food and Nutritional Services (F800, F803, F804, F812)","guidelines":["Verify every tray card against the current diet and liquid-consistency order before serving; never guess a resident's diet.","Thickened-liquid orders apply to all liquids at all times; never provide thin liquids to a resident with a thickened-liquid order.","Keep hot food hot and cold food cold; check and log temperatures per policy; report equipment or holding problems to the supervisor.","Wash hands and follow glove rules in all food handling; staff with vomiting or diarrhea must report illness and not work with food.","Seat residents upright for meals, assist at the resident's pace, and stop and report coughing, wet voice, or pocketing to the licensed nurse."],"roles":{"Licensed Nurse":[{"q":"A resident's diet and liquid-consistency order must be verified before serving any meal, snack, or drink.    ( True / False )","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"A resident on thickened liquids may safely have plain water between meals as long as they drink it slowly.    ( True / False )","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"A resident has the right to refuse a therapeutic diet after being informed of the risks.    ( True / False )","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"A wet or gurgly voice after swallowing can be a sign of aspiration.    ( True / False )","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Coughing during meals is a normal part of aging and does not need to be evaluated.    ( True / False )","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"Foods on a pureed diet should be smooth and require no chewing.    ( True / False )","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"If the kitchen runs out of a menu item, any substitute food may be served regardless of the resident's diet order.    ( True / False )","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"A resident with a nectar-thick (mildly thick) liquid order asks for coffee. The correct action is to:","c":["Serve regular coffee since it is hot, not cold","Serve coffee thickened to the ordered consistency","Deny the coffee entirely","Serve half a cup of regular coffee"],"a":1},{"q":"Which resident has the HIGHEST aspiration risk at meals?","c":["A resident with dysphagia who is being fed quickly while reclined in bed","An independent resident on a regular diet eating in the dining room","A resident on mechanical soft who feeds herself slowly while sitting upright","A resident who prefers to eat small, frequent meals"],"a":0},{"q":"Under F804, food served to residents must be:","c":["Palatable and attractive","At a safe and appetizing temperature","Prepared in a form to meet individual needs","All of the above"],"a":3}],"CNA":[{"q":"Before serving or feeding, check the tray card against the resident and their diet order.    ( True / False )","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Residents should be positioned upright (as close to 90 degrees as tolerated) for meals and remain upright afterward per the care plan.    ( True / False )","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"It is okay to give a resident on thickened liquids a small sip of thin water if they really want it.    ( True / False )","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"When assisting with eating, offer small bites at the resident's pace and make sure the mouth is empty before the next bite.    ( True / False )","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"If a resident coughs repeatedly or has a wet-sounding voice during a meal, stop and tell the licensed nurse.    ( True / False )","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Testing the temperature of a resident's food by tasting it with the serving spoon is acceptable.    ( True / False )","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"Talking to a resident while their mouth is full and encouraging them to hurry helps them finish their meal.    ( True / False )","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"A tray marked “regular diet” is delivered for Resident A, but you know her tray card usually says pureed. What should you do?","c":["Serve the regular tray since it came from the kitchen","Cut the food into very small pieces yourself","Do not serve it — verify the current diet order with the nurse and get the correct tray","Let the resident choose which texture she wants today"],"a":2},{"q":"During lunch, Resident B suddenly coughs hard, eyes watering, but is still able to cough and speak. You should:","c":["Perform abdominal thrusts immediately","Give him water to wash the food down","Slap him on the back while he is upright eating","Stay with him, encourage him to keep coughing, stop the meal, and call for the nurse"],"a":3}],"Dietary Aide":[{"q":"Wearing gloves replaces the need to wash your hands.    ( True / False )","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"Hot foods must be held at safe hot temperatures and cold foods kept cold until served.    ( True / False )","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"If you have vomiting or diarrhea, you must report it to your supervisor and not work with food.    ( True / False )","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"It is okay to send a tray without a tray card if you are sure you remember the resident's diet.    ( True / False )","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"You notice two unlabeled trays — one pureed, one mechanical soft — that look similar. What should you do?","c":["Send them out and let the floor staff figure it out","Guess based on which room they are going to","Stop, check the tray cards/diet list, and confirm with your supervisor before the trays leave the kitchen","Combine them into one tray"],"a":2},{"q":"The safe way to thaw frozen meat for tomorrow's meal is:","c":["On the counter overnight","Under refrigeration","In a sink of warm water","On the steam table set to low"],"a":1}]}},{"id":20,"key":"Pressure_Ulcer_Prevention","month":5,"title":"Prevention of Pressure Ulcers and Skin Breakdown (F686)","guidelines":["Skin risk is assessed on admission, quarterly, with change of condition, and after any new skin finding; interventions are individualized in the care plan.","CNAs observe skin during all routine care (bathing, dressing, incontinence care) and report any redness, open area, blister, or skin complaint to the licensed nurse right away.","Reposition dependent residents per the care plan; float heels; use draw sheets or lifts — never drag a resident across bedding.","Licensed nurses assess, stage, and document new skin findings, notify the provider and responsible party, and update the care plan."],"roles":{"Licensed Nurse":[{"q":"Non-blanchable redness over a bony prominence may indicate a Stage 1 pressure injury.    ( True / False )","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"A pressure injury whose wound bed is obscured by slough or eschar is classified as unstageable.    ( True / False )","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Massaging reddened areas over bony prominences improves circulation and helps prevent pressure injuries.    ( True / False )","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"A deep tissue injury may appear as a purple or maroon area of intact skin.    ( True / False )","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Donut-shaped cushions are recommended devices for pressure redistribution.    ( True / False )","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"Elevating the head of the bed higher than needed increases shear forces on the sacrum.    ( True / False )","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"A resident on a pressure-redistribution mattress no longer needs scheduled repositioning.    ( True / False )","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"During a skin check you find new non-blanchable redness on a resident's heel. Your FIRST clinical actions should be to:","c":["Note it and recheck at the next weekly skin check","Offload the heel completely, assess and document the finding, and notify the provider","Apply a heat pack to improve circulation","Massage the area twice a shift"],"a":1},{"q":"Which resident is at HIGHEST risk for pressure injury?","c":["An independently ambulatory resident with a good appetite","A resident who is bed-bound, incontinent, and eating less than half of meals","A resident who walks with a cane and is continent","A short-stay resident independent in ADLs"],"a":1},{"q":"Braden-type risk assessment considers which of the following?","c":["Mobility and activity level","Moisture and nutrition","Sensory perception and friction/shear","All of the above"],"a":3}],"CNA":[{"q":"CNAs should check the resident's skin during bathing, dressing, and incontinence care.    ( True / False )","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Redness over a bony area that does not fade after pressure is removed should be reported to the nurse right away.    ( True / False )","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Rubbing or massaging a red area over a bony prominence helps it heal faster.    ( True / False )","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"Keeping skin clean and dry after each incontinence episode helps prevent breakdown.    ( True / False )","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"It is acceptable to drag a resident up in bed as long as two staff members help.    ( True / False )","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"Heels can be protected by floating them off the mattress with a pillow placed lengthwise under the calves.    ( True / False )","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Pressure injury prevention is the nurse's job only — CNAs are not responsible.    ( True / False )","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"While bathing Resident A, you notice a purple area of intact skin on the tailbone that was not there yesterday. What should you do?","c":["Finish the bath and mention it at the end of the shift","Cover it with lotion and a brief","Tell the licensed nurse immediately and keep pressure off the area","Massage it gently to bring back circulation"],"a":2},{"q":"A resident in a wheelchair has been sitting in the day room for a long time. Which action helps prevent pressure injury?","c":["Assist with repositioning or weight shifts per the care plan","Make sure the prescribed wheelchair cushion is in place","Offer toileting and check skin when providing care","All of the above"],"a":3}]}},{"id":21,"key":"Oral_Care","month":6,"title":"Oral Care — Daily Oral Hygiene, Denture Care & Dental Referrals (F677, F790/F791)","guidelines":["Provide oral care at least twice daily to every resident who needs assistance — including residents on tube feeding, who are at higher aspiration risk from oral bacteria.","Clean dentures over a towel or basin of water using cool water only; store in a labeled denture cup with water or solution — never in tissues or napkins.","Look at the mouth during every oral care episode and report sores, white patches, broken or loose teeth, bleeding, or pain to the licensed nurse.","Never force oral care on a resistant resident — use a calm, front-facing approach, try again later, and report ongoing refusals so the care plan can be updated."],"roles":{"Licensed Nurse":[{"q":"_____  Poor oral hygiene increases the risk of aspiration pneumonia in dependent residents.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"_____  Residents with no natural teeth do not need routine oral care.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"_____  Dentures should be cleaned with hot water to kill bacteria.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"_____  White patches in the mouth may indicate thrush and should be reported to the physician.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"_____  F677 requires the facility to provide ADL care, including oral hygiene, to residents who cannot perform it themselves.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"_____  Oral care can be skipped for residents receiving tube feeding since they do not eat by mouth.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"_____  Dry mouth is a common side effect of many medications and increases the risk of tooth decay and oral infection.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"A resident's denture cracks during cleaning. The nurse should:","c":["Repair it with household adhesive so the resident can keep using it","Remove it from use if unsafe, document, notify the resident/representative, and initiate a dental referral","Leave it in place and monitor at the next quarterly assessment","Discard it and note the loss in the chart"],"a":1},{"q":"Which resident is at HIGHEST risk for aspiration pneumonia related to oral colonization?","c":["An independent, ambulatory resident who brushes twice daily","A dependent resident with dysphagia who rarely receives oral care","A resident with well-fitting full dentures who performs self-care","A short-stay rehab resident with no swallowing deficits"],"a":1},{"q":"A resident consistently refuses oral care and now has bleeding gums and a foul odor. The BEST next step is to:","c":["Continue documenting the refusals only","Reassess the approach, involve the IDT to revise the care plan, and refer for dental evaluation","Discontinue oral care attempts to respect the refusal","Direct the CNA to complete the care regardless of resistance"],"a":1}],"CNA":[{"q":"_____  Oral care should be provided at least twice a day, and more often if needed.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"_____  When cleaning dentures, hold them over a towel or a basin of water so they will not break if dropped.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"_____  Dentures should be rinsed and stored dry overnight.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"_____  You should look at the resident's mouth during oral care and report sores, white patches, or broken teeth to the nurse.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"_____  Residents who do not eat by mouth (tube feeding) do not need mouth care.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"_____  If a resident refuses oral care, try again later using a calm approach — and report ongoing refusals to the nurse.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"_____  Hot water is best for cleaning dentures.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"While brushing Resident A's teeth, you notice white patches on the tongue and inside the cheeks. What should you do?","c":["Scrub the patches harder to remove them","Finish care gently and report the finding to the nurse","Nothing — white patches are normal in older adults","Rinse with mouthwash; that will clear the patches"],"a":1},{"q":"A resident with dementia clamps their mouth shut and turns away when you approach with a toothbrush. What should you do FIRST?","c":["Gently force the toothbrush in so the care gets done","Pause, approach from the front at eye level, explain simply, and try hand-over-hand — or come back later","Skip oral care for this resident permanently","Ask another CNA to hold the resident's head still"],"a":1}]}},{"id":22,"key":"Sexual_Unlawful_Harassment","month":6,"title":"Sexual / Unlawful Harassment Prevention","guidelines":["All employees must complete state-required harassment prevention training (1 hour nonsupervisory / 2 hours supervisory) every 2 years per SB 1343.","Report harassment through any channel — supervisor, DON, Administrator, or HR. No chain of command is required, and reports may be verbal or written.","Retaliation against anyone who reports harassment in good faith or participates in an investigation is strictly prohibited.","Harassment by residents or visitors must be reported so protective interventions can be put in place; harassment of a resident is abuse and must be reported under the abuse policy."],"roles":{"All Staff":[{"q":"_____  Harassment can come from a supervisor, a coworker, a resident, or a visitor — not only from someone in authority.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"_____  For conduct to count as unlawful sexual harassment, it must include physical touching.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"_____  Under California law, non-supervisory employees must receive at least one hour of harassment prevention training every two years.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"_____  If offensive jokes or comments were meant as \"just kidding,\" they cannot create a hostile work environment.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"_____  An employee who reports harassment in good faith is protected from retaliation, even if the investigation does not confirm the complaint.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"_____  Only the person directly targeted by the harassment is allowed to report it.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"Which situation BEST describes quid pro quo harassment?","c":["A coworker repeatedly tells offensive jokes in the break room","A supervisor hints that a better schedule depends on going on a date with them","A visitor makes one rude comment to the receptionist","Two employees loudly disagree about an assignment"],"a":1},{"q":"A resident repeatedly makes sexual comments and tries to touch a CNA during care. What should the CNA do?","c":["Ignore it — it is part of the job in a skilled nursing facility","Refuse to care for the resident without telling anyone","Argue with the resident until the behavior stops","Report it to the charge nurse/supervisor so the team can put protective interventions in place"],"a":3}]}},{"id":23,"key":"Trauma_Informed_Care","month":6,"title":"Trauma-Informed Care (F699)","guidelines":["Approach residents from the front, explain each step before touching, and ask permission — every resident, every time.","Offer real choices in care (timing, method, caregiver where possible) and never force care on a distressed resident; stop, reassure, and re-approach.","Do not ask residents to recount details of their trauma; trauma-informed care does not require knowing the story.","Report new or worsening fear, refusals, or distress during care to the licensed nurse the same shift so the care plan can be updated."],"roles":{"Licensed Nurse":[{"q":"_____  F699 requires facilities to provide culturally competent, trauma-informed care that accounts for residents' experiences and preferences.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"_____  To provide trauma-informed care, staff must first get the resident to describe the details of their traumatic experience.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"_____  Behaviors such as refusing a shower or striking out during care may be trauma responses rather than intentional aggression.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"_____  Trauma-informed care applies only to residents with a formally documented PTSD diagnosis.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"_____  Offering choices — such as when to bathe or who provides care — is a core trauma-informed intervention.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"_____  Encouraging a distressed resident to \"just forget the past\" is an effective trauma intervention.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"_____  Trauma history and individualized approaches should be incorporated into the resident's care plan.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"A resident who is a combat veteran becomes agitated and defensive when a CNA approaches him from behind. The BEST trauma-informed response is to:","c":["Document the episode as aggressive behavior requiring monitoring","Coach staff to approach from the front, use his name, and explain care before touching","Request a PRN antipsychotic order for agitation","Assign two staff to hold him steady during care"],"a":1},{"q":"Which of the following is a core principle of trauma-informed care?","c":["Confronting residents about unreasonable fears","Safety, choice, and collaboration","Strict enforcement of the facility routine","Detailed trauma debriefing at admission"],"a":1},{"q":"A resident with a documented history of abuse becomes tearful and withdrawn during perineal care. The nurse should:","c":["Continue quickly so the care is finished sooner","Stop, reassure the resident, offer choices about how and when care continues, and update the care plan","Discontinue all perineal care for this resident","Tell the resident the care is required by facility policy"],"a":1}],"CNA":[{"q":"_____  Trauma can affect how a resident reacts to everyday care, such as bathing and dressing.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"_____  If a resident refuses a shower, the best response is to insist firmly so the task gets done.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"_____  Telling the resident what you are going to do before you touch them helps them feel safe.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"_____  Only residents who talk about their past trauma can be affected by it.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"_____  Approaching a resident from the front and calling them by name can prevent startling them.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"_____  A CNA should ask the resident detailed questions about their trauma so they can understand it better.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"_____  Reporting a resident's new fear or distress during care to the nurse is part of the CNA's job.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"During a bed bath, Resident A suddenly becomes fearful, cries, and pushes your hands away. What should you do FIRST?","c":["Finish the bath quickly so it is over sooner","Stop, speak calmly, give the resident a moment, and offer to continue later or in a different way","Get another CNA to help hold the resident still","Skip baths for this resident from now on"],"a":1},{"q":"A resident who survived a house fire becomes very anxious every time the fire alarm is tested. What should you do?","c":["Tell the resident that alarms are no big deal","Stay close, reassure and comfort the resident, and report it to the nurse so it can be added to the care plan","Move the resident to their room and shut the door without explanation","Nothing — anxiety during alarms is normal for everyone"],"a":1}]}},{"id":24,"key":"Bowel_Bladder_Management","month":7,"title":"Bowel and Bladder Training, Management & Fecal Impaction (F690)","guidelines":["Continence is assessed on admission and with change of condition; new incontinence is reported as a change of condition, not just managed with briefs.","Toileting programs (scheduled toileting, prompted voiding) are followed as care-planned; call lights are answered promptly to prevent episodes and falls.","Catheter care: closed system, bag below bladder, secured tubing, daily peri/catheter care; UTI warning signs including new confusion are reported immediately.","Bowel movements are documented every shift; no BM in 3 days (or per protocol) is reported. Frequent small liquid stools may signal impaction, not simple diarrhea."],"roles":{"Licensed Nurse":[{"q":"T / F — Each resident's continence status must be assessed on admission and with any change in condition.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — An indwelling urinary catheter may be continued simply because the resident was admitted with one.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — Liquid stool leaking around impacted stool (paradoxical diarrhea) can be a sign of fecal impaction.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — Cloudy or strong-smelling urine alone confirms a UTI and requires antibiotics.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — Prompted voiding and scheduled toileting programs can restore or improve continence for many residents.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — The catheter drainage bag should be kept below the level of the bladder at all times.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — A resident on the bowel protocol with no BM for several days does not need provider notification as long as they are eating.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"A previously continent resident develops new urinary incontinence. The nurse's FIRST action should be to:","c":["Apply incontinence briefs and update the care plan","Assess for reversible causes — UTI, new medications, constipation/impaction, mobility changes","Request an order for an indwelling catheter","Restrict evening fluids"],"a":1},{"q":"Which cluster of findings most suggests fecal impaction?","c":["Formed bowel movements daily with good appetite","No formed BM for days, abdominal distension, and frequent small liquid stools or seepage","Increased appetite and increased activity","Clear yellow urine and stable vital signs"],"a":1},{"q":"Which practice set BEST prevents catheter-associated urinary tract infection (CAUTI)?","c":["Disconnect the drainage bag during ambulation for convenience","Maintain a closed system, secure the tubing, keep the bag below the bladder, and perform daily peri/catheter care","Irrigate the catheter routinely every shift","Change the catheter every week regardless of condition"],"a":1}],"CNA":[{"q":"T / F — Incontinence is a normal, expected part of aging that cannot be improved.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — Toileting residents on a schedule can prevent incontinence episodes.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — The catheter drainage bag may be laid on the bed next to the resident during transport.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — You should report to the nurse if a resident has had no bowel movement in 3 days (or per facility protocol).","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — Peri-care for female residents should always be performed wiping front to back.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — Frequent small liquid stools always mean diarrhea and never constipation.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — Prompt incontinence care protects both the resident's skin and their dignity.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"While emptying a catheter bag you notice Resident A's urine is dark and cloudy, and the resident seems newly confused. What should you do?","c":["Empty the bag, record the amount, and move on","Report your findings to the nurse right away — this could be a urinary tract infection","Encourage fluids and check again tomorrow","Nothing — dark urine is normal with a catheter"],"a":1},{"q":"A resident on a toileting program frequently asks for help to the bathroom while staff are busy. What is the BEST response?","c":["Tell the resident to use their brief this time","Answer promptly and keep the toileting schedule — delays cause incontinence episodes and falls","Limit the resident's fluids so they need the toilet less often","Ask the resident to wait until after meal trays are passed"],"a":1}]}},{"id":25,"key":"Dementia_4_Actions_Reactions","month":7,"title":"Dementia Series 4 of 5: Actions & Reactions","guidelines":["Treat behavior as communication: look for pain, toileting needs, hunger, overstimulation, fatigue, or fear before anything else.","Use the ABC approach — identify the antecedent, describe the behavior objectively, note the consequence — and report patterns.","Nonpharmacological interventions come first: calm approach from the front, stop-and-return, redirection, reduced stimulation, consistent caregivers.","Never adopt a chemical-restraint mindset. Report new or worsening behaviors, suspected pain, and sudden confusion to the nurse the same day; safety risks immediately."],"roles":{"Licensed Nurse":[{"q":"T / F — Behavior in dementia is often a form of communication about an unmet need.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — Pain is a common and frequently missed trigger for behavioral expressions in residents with dementia.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — The first-line response to a new behavioral symptom should be a PRN antipsychotic.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — In the ABC approach, “A” stands for Antecedent — what happened right before the behavior.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — A medication used to sedate a resident for staff convenience is considered a chemical restraint.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — Behaviors that appear suddenly along with new confusion should be assumed to be dementia progression.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — Once an effective nonpharmacological intervention is found, it does not need to be documented in the care plan.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"A resident becomes agitated around 5:00 PM most evenings. Using the ABC approach, the FIRST step is to:","c":["Request a PRN anxiolytic order for the evening","Identify the antecedents — what happens before 5:00 PM (shift change noise, hunger, fatigue, less staff presence)","Move the resident to their room at 4:30 PM each day","Chart “behaviors noted” each evening"],"a":1},{"q":"Which finding should prompt an assessment for PAIN as the trigger for a behavior?","c":["Grimacing, guarding, or striking out during repositioning or transfers","Behavior that occurs only during family visits","Improved appetite at meals","Sleeping well through the night"],"a":0},{"q":"Which of the following is an example of a CHEMICAL restraint?","c":["A scheduled antidepressant for diagnosed depression with documented monitoring","A PRN antipsychotic given for non-dangerous wandering because staff are busy","An analgesic given before painful wound care","An anticonvulsant given for a seizure disorder"],"a":1}],"CNA":[{"q":"T / F — When a resident with dementia hits out during care, it is usually intentional and personal.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — A behavior often means the resident has an unmet need, such as pain, hunger, or needing to toilet.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — Overstimulation — noise, crowds, loud TV — can trigger behaviors in residents with dementia.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — Arguing with or correcting the resident is a good way to stop a behavior.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — CNAs should report new or worsening behaviors to the nurse right away.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — If your first approach fails, stepping away briefly and returning later with a calm approach can work.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — Behaviors only need to be reported if someone gets hurt.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"Resident A yells and grabs your arm during morning care. What should you do FIRST?","c":["Keep going so you can finish the task quickly","Stop the task, step back, speak calmly, and try again in a few minutes","Firmly tell the resident to stop grabbing you","Ask another CNA to hold the resident's hands so you can finish"],"a":1},{"q":"A resident paces the hallway and tries door handles every day right before lunch. What could this behavior be telling you?","c":["Nothing — pacing is random in dementia","The resident may be hungry, need to toilet, or be restless — an unmet need to report and address","The resident wants attention and should be ignored","The dementia is getting worse and nothing will help"],"a":1}]}},{"id":26,"key":"Resident_Activities","month":7,"title":"Resident Activities (F679)","guidelines":["Activity plans are individualized from the activity assessment (interests, history, preferences, abilities) and updated with changes in condition.","All staff — not just the Activities department — invite, prepare, escort, and engage residents; bedbound residents receive in-room and one-to-one programming.","Dementia-appropriate engagement uses failure-free, sensory, and short activities built on remaining abilities.","Refusals, withdrawal, isolation, or mood changes are reported to the Activities department and the nurse for care-plan follow-up."],"roles":{"All Staff":[{"q":"T / F — Activities are only the responsibility of the Activities Department.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — A resident's activity plan must be based on that resident's own interests, preferences, and abilities.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — Residents who are bedbound cannot participate in the activities program.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — Escorting or transporting a resident to an activity is a way every staff member can support the program.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — If a resident declines an activity once, staff should stop offering activities to that resident.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — Meaningful engagement can reduce boredom, loneliness, and behavioral symptoms in residents with dementia.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Which of the following is an appropriate in-room activity for a bedbound resident?","c":["Turning on the TV and leaving the room","Music the resident enjoys, hand massage, or a one-to-one visit","Waiting until the resident is strong enough to attend group activities","Nothing — bedbound residents are excluded from the program"],"a":1},{"q":"A resident with dementia comes into the activity room but will not sit down. What is the BEST response?","c":["Escort the resident back to their room","Firmly tell the resident to sit down","Offer a familiar, failure-free task (folding towels, music) they can do while standing or moving","Call the nurse to request a PRN medication"],"a":2}]}},{"id":27,"key":"Slips_Trips_Falls_Staff","month":7,"title":"Slips, Trips and Falls — Staff Safety","guidelines":["Spill response ownership: the employee who finds a spill guards or marks it immediately and stays until it is cleaned or handed off — no walking past.","Wet floor signs are placed before mopping at all approaches and removed promptly when the floor is dry.","Cords never cross walkways; hallways stay clear; burned-out lights and other hazards are reported to the supervisor or Maintenance.","All work injuries and near-misses are reported to the supervisor the same shift; workers' compensation claim forms are provided upon report of injury. Reporting is protected — no retaliation."],"roles":{"All Staff":[{"q":"T / F — If you see a spill, it is your job to make it safe — no matter what department you work in.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — Once a wet floor sign is up, the spill can wait until housekeeping is available.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — Near-misses — like slipping without falling — should be reported so the hazard can be fixed.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — Running a cord across a walkway is acceptable as long as staff know it is there.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — Slip-resistant footwear reduces your risk of falling on wet floors.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — Work injuries only need to be reported if they are serious enough to see a doctor.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"You find a puddle in the hallway. What should you do FIRST?","c":["Walk around it and continue with your task","Guard or mark the hazard right away and make sure it gets cleaned — do not leave it unattended","Mention it to housekeeping at the end of your shift","Lay a paper towel over it and move on"],"a":1},{"q":"Under the facility's Injury and Illness Prevention Program (IIPP), reporting safety hazards is:","c":["Only the Maintenance department's job","Optional, if you have time","Every employee's responsibility, protected from retaliation","Only a supervisor's responsibility"],"a":2}]}},{"id":28,"key":"Accident_Prevention_O2_Safety","month":8,"title":"Accident Prevention & Safety Measures — Oxygen Storage/Handling","guidelines":["Cylinders are secured upright in racks, stands, or approved carts at all times — never free-standing, leaning, or lying loose.","Full and empty cylinders are stored in separate, clearly labeled areas, away from heat, flame, and combustibles.","Only licensed nurses adjust oxygen flow rates; all staff verify tubing placement and report low gauges, hazards, and equipment problems immediately.","Unsafe equipment (frayed cords, damaged regulators, malfunctioning concentrators) is removed from service, tagged, and reported to maintenance the same day."],"roles":{"Licensed Nurse":[{"q":"T   F   Oxygen is a flammable gas that can explode on its own.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T   F   Full and empty cylinders must be stored in separate, clearly labeled areas.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T   F   The nurse must verify the resident's oxygen flow rate against the physician's order every shift and after any transport.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T   F   A cylinder may be left standing unsecured in a resident's room as long as staff will return within a few minutes.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T   F   Long oxygen tubing crossing a walkway is a trip hazard and must be re-routed or secured.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T   F   Petroleum-based products such as petroleum jelly are safe to apply around the nose of a resident receiving oxygen.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T   F   Concentrator filters must be checked and cleaned per the manufacturer's schedule, and the unit kept clear of walls and curtains.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"You find a portable cylinder leaning upright against the wall in the hallway. Your FIRST action should be:","c":["Leave it — it is upright and not in anyone's way","Secure it immediately in an approved rack, stand, or cart","Lay it flat on the floor so it cannot fall","Ask housekeeping to move it at the end of the shift"],"a":1},{"q":"A resident's order reads O2 at 2 L/min via nasal cannula, but you find the flowmeter set at 4 L/min. The best action is:","c":["Leave it — more oxygen is safer than less","Return the flow to 2 L/min, assess the resident, determine who changed it and why, notify the provider if indicated, and document","Turn the oxygen off until the provider calls back","Ask the CNA to watch the resident and recheck it tomorrow"],"a":1},{"q":"During rounds you notice a concentrator with a frayed power cord that is still running. You should:","c":["Wrap the frayed section with tape and continue use","Note it in the maintenance log for review at the next preventive-maintenance date","Switch the resident to an alternate oxygen source, remove the unit from service, tag it, and report it to maintenance","Unplug it only at night when staffing is lower"],"a":2}],"CNA":[{"q":"T   F   Smoking, vaping, and open flames are never allowed anywhere oxygen is in use or stored.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T   F   If a resident on oxygen says they cannot breathe, the CNA should turn the flow rate up to help them.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T   F   Oxygen cylinders must be kept secured upright in a rack, stand, or holder at all times.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T   F   An empty cylinder is harmless and can be stored anywhere that is convenient.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T   F   During care, the CNA should check that oxygen tubing is not caught under wheelchair wheels, bed wheels, or side rails.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T   F   If you see a frayed electrical cord on an oxygen concentrator, keep it running and mention it at the end of your shift.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T   F   The CNA should tell the nurse right away if a resident's cannula is off or the cylinder gauge is reading low.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"You are taking a resident to therapy in a wheelchair with portable oxygen. The safest way to transport the cylinder is:","c":["Lay the cylinder across the resident's lap so they can hold it","Place the cylinder in the wheelchair's cylinder holder, secured, and check the gauge before leaving","Have the resident carry the cylinder in a tote bag on the armrest","Roll the cylinder alongside the wheelchair"],"a":1},{"q":"A visitor takes out a lighter next to a resident who is on oxygen. You should:","c":["Say nothing — visitors are not staff, so the rules do not apply to you","Move the resident's cannula away from the visitor","Politely ask the visitor to stop immediately, explain oxygen is in use, and notify the nurse","Open a window and let the visitor continue"],"a":2}]}},{"id":30,"key":"Choking_Prevention_Intervention","month":8,"title":"Choking Prevention and Intervention","guidelines":["Verify every tray against the diet card before serving; wrong-texture trays are held and corrected — never served 'just this once.'","Residents are positioned upright (as close to 90 degrees as possible) for all eating and drinking, and supervised at the level specified in the care plan.","Severe choking (cannot cough, speak, or breathe) triggers immediate response: shout for help, abdominal thrusts per training, CPR and 911 if the resident becomes unresponsive. No blind finger sweeps.","After any choking event: nursing assessment and monitoring, provider and responsible-party notification, incident report, and IDT review of diet and supervision."],"roles":{"Licensed Nurse":[{"q":"T   F   A resident who is coughing forcefully should be encouraged to keep coughing rather than receiving immediate abdominal thrusts.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T   F   A choking resident who cannot cough, speak, or breathe requires immediate intervention.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T   F   A choking resident will always make noise, so silent choking is not a real risk.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T   F   If a choking resident becomes unresponsive, lower them to a firm surface, activate 911/code response, and begin CPR.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T   F   Blind finger sweeps are recommended to remove an object you cannot see.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T   F   After a successful choking rescue, no further assessment is needed if the resident says they feel fine.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T   F   Thickened liquids and texture-modified foods must match the resident's current diet order.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"At lunch, a resident begins coughing weakly, makes a high-pitched sound, and their face is turning dusky. You should:","c":["Encourage them to keep coughing and observe — some air is moving","Treat this as severe obstruction: call for help and begin abdominal thrusts now","Offer sips of water to wash the food down","Lay them flat and wait for the cough to clear"],"a":1},{"q":"A tray of regular-texture food is delivered for a resident whose order reads pureed with nectar-thick liquids. The correct action is:","c":["Serve it — one meal at the wrong texture rarely causes harm","Serve only the softer items from the tray","Hold the tray, obtain the correct texture from dietary, and flag the discrepancy","Let the resident choose which texture they prefer today"],"a":2},{"q":"After a resident is successfully rescued from choking, which of the following is required?","c":["Nursing assessment and continued monitoring","Provider and responsible-party notification","Incident report and IDT review of diet and supervision","All of the above"],"a":3}],"CNA":[{"q":"T   F   Residents should be seated upright, as close to 90 degrees as possible, for all meals and drinks.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T   F   If a resident's tray has the wrong food texture, it is OK to serve it as long as the resident is hungry.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T   F   The universal sign of choking is hands clutched to the throat.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T   F   A resident who can cough forcefully and talk is still moving air — stay with them and encourage coughing.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T   F   Feeding residents quickly helps them finish their meals safely.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T   F   Silent choking (no cough, no voice) is less dangerous than loud coughing.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T   F   Any new coughing, throat-clearing, or 'gurgly' voice during meals should be reported to the nurse the same shift.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"At dinner, a resident suddenly grabs their throat and cannot speak or cough. You should:","c":["Run to the nurses' station and wait there for the nurse","Shout for help so the nurse is summoned, and immediately assist the resident according to your choking-response training","Offer water to help the food go down","Pat them gently on the back and continue serving other residents"],"a":1},{"q":"A family member brings regular coffee for a resident who is on nectar-thick liquids. You should:","c":["Serve the coffee — family requests override the diet order","Serve half the cup so everyone is happy","Politely explain the resident's liquids must be thickened for safety, hold the coffee, and notify the nurse","Pour the coffee into a spill-proof mug and hand it over"],"a":2}]}},{"id":29,"key":"Cardiopulmonary_Distress_Signs","month":8,"title":"Signs and Symptoms of Cardiopulmonary Distress","guidelines":["Any staff member who notices a change — eating less, more confused, more tired, breathing differently — reports it to the licensed nurse the same shift using Stop and Watch. Never wait until end of shift.","Late/severe signs (cyanosis, accessory-muscle breathing, chest pain, altered mental status) trigger emergency-level response: stay with the resident, position upright, and escalate immediately.","Licensed nurses give organized SBAR reports to the provider with vitals, baseline comparison, and code status in hand, and document all notifications.","A full-code resident who is unresponsive without normal breathing/pulse receives immediate CPR and 911 per F678; a DNR resident still receives full assessment and treatment of the underlying problem."],"roles":{"Licensed Nurse":[{"q":"T   F   An increased respiratory rate is often one of the earliest measurable signs of respiratory distress.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T   F   Cyanosis is an early sign of hypoxia.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T   F   New confusion or restlessness in an elderly resident can signal hypoxia even when the resident does not complain of shortness of breath.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T   F   If a resident has a DNR order, staff should not assess or treat their breathing problems.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T   F   SBAR stands for Situation, Background, Assessment, Recommendation.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T   F   Cardiac events in elderly residents always present as crushing substernal chest pain.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T   F   A full-code resident found unresponsive with no normal breathing or pulse requires immediate CPR and activation of 911.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"A resident develops sudden shortness of breath. After calling for help, the nurse's best FIRST bedside actions are:","c":["Leave to call the provider from the nurses' station","Stay with the resident, raise the head of bed upright, obtain vitals with SpO2, and apply oxygen per order/protocol","Lay the resident flat and elevate the legs","Give a PRN anxiolytic and reassess in 30 minutes"],"a":1},{"q":"Which combination of findings most clearly indicates LATE, severe distress requiring emergency escalation?","c":["Respiratory rate 22, resident says they feel a little tired","New anxiety with slightly elevated heart rate","Cyanotic lips, use of accessory muscles, and decreasing level of consciousness","SpO2 at the resident's known baseline with mild exertional dyspnea"],"a":2},{"q":"The primary purpose of the Stop and Watch tool is to:","c":["Replace the licensed nurse's assessment","Give all staff a structured way to flag subtle changes in condition and get them to the nurse early","Document falls for the QAPI committee","Track meal intake percentages for dietary"],"a":1}],"CNA":[{"q":"T   F   CNAs are often the first to notice small changes because they spend the most hands-on time with residents.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T   F   If a resident just seems 'a little more tired than usual,' it is not worth reporting.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T   F   Bluish lips or fingertips are an emergency-level sign — tell the nurse immediately.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T   F   A resident who ate and drank less than usual should be reported to the nurse using Stop and Watch.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T   F   If a resident says 'I can't catch my breath,' you should lay them flat so they can rest.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T   F   Changes you notice during your shift should be saved up and reported at the end of the shift.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T   F   If a resident is in distress, stay with them and call for the nurse — do not leave them alone.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"During morning care, a resident who is normally alert seems confused and is breathing faster than usual. You should:","c":["Finish care and mention it at the end of your shift","Stop, stay with the resident, and report the change to the nurse right away","Give the resident some water and let them rest","Chart it in the meal-intake record"],"a":1},{"q":"A resident suddenly clutches their chest and their skin looks pale and sweaty. Your BEST response is:","c":["Help them walk to the day room where staff can watch them","Bring them a snack in case it is low blood sugar","Stay with them, call for the nurse immediately (call light/shout for help), and help them into a comfortable upright position","Take them outside for fresh air"],"a":2}]}},{"id":32,"key":"Dementia_5_Making_A_Difference","month":9,"title":"Dementia Series 5 of 5: Making a Difference","guidelines":["Learn each resident's story (history, preferences, routines) and use it to guide care and engagement.","Support remaining abilities — cue and assist rather than take over, and build meaningful moments into daily care.","Partner with families: listen, share positive moments, and ask what works at home; involve Social Services for support.","If you feel frustrated during care, pause, make sure the resident is safe, step back, and ask a coworker or nurse for help — then report behavior changes to the licensed nurse."],"roles":{"Licensed Nurse":[{"q":"_____  Person-centered dementia care focuses on the resident's remaining abilities, not only on deficits.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"_____  Once a resident reaches advanced dementia, meaningful engagement is no longer possible.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"_____  Knowing a resident's life story can help staff prevent and de-escalate distress behaviors.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"_____  Family members should be discouraged from participating in care because it interferes with facility routines.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"_____  CMS requires nurse aide in-service training to include dementia management.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"_____  Caregiver stress is a personal problem that should not be discussed at work.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"_____  Even residents with late-stage dementia can experience comfort through music, touch, and tone of voice.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"A resident with moderate dementia becomes distressed every evening around dinner time. The BEST first approach is to:","c":["Request a PRN antipsychotic order from the provider","Review the resident's life story and routines and trial individualized, non-drug approaches","Move the resident to their room until the behavior stops","Restrict the resident from the dining room"],"a":1},{"q":"Which is the best example of celebrating a resident's remaining abilities?","c":["Performing all ADLs for the resident to save time","Inviting the resident to fold washcloths, a task she enjoys and can still do","Quizzing the resident on dates and names to exercise memory","Correcting the resident each time she is confused"],"a":1},{"q":"A daughter visits her mother and leaves in tears, saying \"She doesn't even know me anymore.\" The nurse should:","c":["Suggest she visit less often since visits are upsetting","Listen with empathy, offer information about dementia progression and visit strategies, and involve Social Services for support","Reassure her that her mother will improve","Refer the family to the ombudsman"],"a":1}],"CNA":[{"q":"_____  Learning about a resident's past — their job, hobbies, and family — helps you give better care.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"_____  If a resident cannot remember your name, spending time with them doesn't really matter.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"_____  Short, simple moments — a song, a hand massage, looking at photos — count as meaningful engagement.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"_____  You should do all ADLs for residents with dementia even when they can still do parts themselves.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"_____  Telling the nurse about a change in a resident's mood or behavior is part of a CNA's job.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"_____  Feeling frustrated while caring for residents with dementia means you are a bad caregiver.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"_____  Families often know calming techniques that work — asking them is good practice.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Resident A, a retired farmer, becomes restless and tries to get up every morning at 5 AM. What is the BEST response?","c":["Tell him firmly to go back to bed","Offer an early-morning routine that fits his lifelong habit and report the pattern to the nurse","Close his door so he does not disturb others","Ask the nurse to request a sleeping medication"],"a":1},{"q":"During morning care, a resident with dementia hits at you and you feel overwhelmed. What should you do?","c":["Continue the care quickly so it is over sooner","Pause, make sure the resident is safe, step back, ask a coworker or the nurse for help, and try again later","Raise your voice so the resident understands you","Skip the care and not tell anyone"],"a":1}]}},{"id":33,"key":"Hazard_Communication_SDS","month":9,"title":"Hazard Communication (SDS — Your Right to Know)","guidelines":["Know where your department's SDS binder / electronic SDS access is located and how to find first-aid measures on an SDS.","Every secondary container (spray bottle, bucket) must be labeled with the product name and hazard warning — never use or leave an unlabeled container.","Never mix chemicals. Bleach mixed with ammonia releases toxic gas.","For eye or skin exposure, flush immediately with water per the SDS and report the exposure to your supervisor right away."],"roles":{"All Staff":[{"q":"_____  Under Cal/OSHA's Hazard Communication standard, you have the right to know about the hazards of the chemicals you work with.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"_____  It is safe to mix bleach and ammonia-based cleaners as long as the area is well ventilated.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"_____  A secondary container (such as a spray bottle) never needs a label as long as the original container is somewhere in the building.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"_____  The skull-and-crossbones pictogram warns that a chemical can be toxic or fatal.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"_____  If a chemical splashes into your eyes, you should finish your task first and rinse your eyes when it is convenient.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"_____  Safety Data Sheets must be readily accessible to staff on all shifts.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Where do you find first-aid information for a chemical product used at the facility?","c":["Ask a coworker who has used the product before","The Safety Data Sheet (SDS) for that product","The product's purchase order","The maintenance work order log"],"a":1},{"q":"You find an unlabeled spray bottle of blue liquid in the housekeeping closet. What should you do?","c":["Smell it carefully to try to identify it","Use it — blue liquid is usually glass cleaner","Pour it down the drain to get rid of it","Do not use it; report it to your supervisor so it can be identified and labeled or discarded"],"a":3}]}},{"id":31,"key":"Antibiotic_Stewardship","month":9,"title":"Infection Control: Antibiotic Stewardship","guidelines":["Colonization (bacteria present without signs/symptoms) is not treated with antibiotics; infection (new or worsening signs/symptoms) is assessed and reported promptly.","Foul-smelling, dark, or cloudy urine alone is often dehydration — hydrate, monitor, and report; it is not by itself an indication for antibiotics.","Licensed nurses report complete, objective SBAR findings (vitals, localized symptoms, mental status change from baseline) so providers can follow stewardship protocols.","For residents on antibiotics, report new diarrhea promptly (possible C. diff) and use soap-and-water hand hygiene."],"roles":{"Licensed Nurse":[{"q":"_____  F881 requires the facility to have an antibiotic stewardship program that includes antibiotic use protocols and a system to monitor antibiotic use.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"_____  A positive urine culture always means the resident has a urinary tract infection that requires antibiotics.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"_____  Colonization means bacteria are present without causing signs or symptoms of infection.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"_____  Foul-smelling or cloudy urine alone is an indication for antibiotics.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"_____  Watchful waiting with scheduled monitoring and hydration can be an appropriate plan for some suspected infections.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"_____  Antibiotic overuse contributes to C. difficile infection and multidrug-resistant organisms.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"_____  When calling a provider about a suspected UTI, the nurse should report only the culture result, not the clinical signs and symptoms.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"A resident has new painful urination, a temperature of 101.2°F, and new suprapubic tenderness. The nurse's BEST action is to:","c":["Wait until the next scheduled provider visit to mention it","Use SBAR to report the specific signs, symptoms, and vital signs to the provider now","Request a broad-spectrum antibiotic without completing an assessment","Encourage fluids only and reassess in one week"],"a":1},{"q":"A family insists on antibiotics for a resident whose urine culture is positive but who has no symptoms. The BEST response is to:","c":["Ask the provider to prescribe antibiotics to satisfy the family","Explain colonization versus infection and the risks of unnecessary antibiotics, describe the monitoring plan, and involve the provider","Tell the family the facility does not use antibiotics","Avoid the family until they stop asking"],"a":1},{"q":"Which statement BEST describes the goal of antibiotic stewardship?","c":["Eliminating all antibiotic use in the facility","The right antibiotic, at the right dose, for the right duration — only when infection is likely","Reserving antibiotics for residents whose families request them","Starting antibiotics for every positive culture within 24 hours"],"a":1}],"CNA":[{"q":"_____  Reporting exactly what you observe — new pain with urination, fever, or a change in alertness — helps the nurse and provider decide whether antibiotics are needed.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"_____  Encouraging fluids is one way CNAs support residents who are being monitored for a possible infection.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"_____  Dark or strong-smelling urine always means the resident has an infection.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"_____  Antibiotics are harmless, so it is better for a resident to take them \"just in case.\"","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"_____  A new or sudden change in a resident's alertness or confusion should be reported to the nurse right away.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"_____  If a resident is on \"watchful waiting,\" staff no longer need to monitor them.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"_____  Diarrhea caused by C. diff can be triggered by antibiotic use.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Resident B's urine smells strong today, but she has no fever, no pain, and is acting like her normal self. What should you do?","c":["Tell the nurse the resident needs antibiotics","Offer fluids throughout your shift and report your exact observation to the nurse","Do nothing — strong-smelling urine is never important","Tell the family the resident has a UTI"],"a":1},{"q":"A resident who started antibiotics two days ago has had three loose stools this shift. What should you do?","c":["Record it and report it to the nurse promptly, and use soap and water for hand hygiene","Wait until tomorrow to see if it continues","Give an over-the-counter anti-diarrheal medication","Nothing — loose stools are normal with antibiotics and never need reporting"],"a":0}]}},{"id":34,"key":"Lockout_Tagout","month":9,"title":"Lock Out / Tag Out","guidelines":["Never remove, bypass, or ignore a lock or tag on any equipment — only the authorized person who applied it may remove it.","If equipment you need is locked or tagged out, use the backup process and notify your supervisor — do not use the equipment.","Take malfunctioning equipment out of service, label it, and report it to Maintenance and your supervisor via the work-order process.","Unplugging equipment does not remove all hazardous energy — servicing is Maintenance's role, not yours."],"roles":{"All Staff":[{"q":"_____  Only authorized, trained employees — typically Maintenance — may apply or remove lockout/tagout devices.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"_____  If a tagged machine looks fine and you are in a hurry, it is OK to use it as long as you are careful.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"_____  You may remove someone else's lock if you put it back when you are done.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"_____  Most Morning Star staff are \"affected\" employees — your job is to recognize LOTO devices and leave the equipment alone.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"_____  Unplugging a machine yourself always removes all hazardous energy and makes it safe to service.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"_____  Malfunctioning equipment should be taken out of service and reported to Maintenance rather than used until it completely fails.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"You find a lock and tag on the dish machine in the kitchen. What should you do?","c":["Remove the tag if Maintenance has already gone home","Leave the lock and tag in place, do not use the machine, and follow the backup process","Run one quick load and then stop using it","Ask a coworker to remove the lock for you"],"a":1},{"q":"A mechanical resident lift stops working during your shift. What should you do?","c":["Keep using it carefully for lighter residents only","Take it out of service, label it, and report it to Maintenance and your supervisor","Attempt to repair it yourself","Leave it in the hallway for the next shift to deal with"],"a":1}]}},{"id":35,"key":"Death_Dying_Hospice","month":10,"title":"Death and Dying — Hospice","guidelines":["Report signs of approaching death and any change in condition to the licensed nurse — including for hospice residents.","Provide frequent mouth and lip care, gentle repositioning, and skin protection for dying residents; report nonverbal pain cues (grimacing, moaning, restlessness) immediately.","Continue to speak gently to dying residents — hearing is believed to be one of the last senses to fade.","Code status is verified from the POLST and physician orders by the licensed nurse — never assume or guess.","Provide postmortem care with privacy and dignity, honoring cultural and religious practices whenever possible.","Support roommates and grieving residents without sharing clinical details; seek support for your own grief through your supervisor and facility resources."],"roles":{"Licensed Nurse":[{"q":"When a resident elects hospice, the facility remains responsible for room and board and day-to-day ADL and nursing care.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"A POLST is only a suggestion, so a full code must always be initiated until the family arrives and decides.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"Mottled, cool extremities and irregular breathing with pauses are common signs of approaching death.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Once a resident is on hospice, the facility nurse no longer assesses or documents on that resident.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"Hearing is believed to be one of the last senses to fade, so staff should continue speaking to dying residents.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Pain assessment stops when a dying resident can no longer verbalize their pain.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"Care of the body after death should reflect the family's cultural and religious preferences whenever possible.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"A hospice resident who is actively dying develops noisy terminal secretions and is grimacing with repositioning. The nurse's BEST action is to:","c":["Do nothing — these are natural parts of dying and require no response","Assess for discomfort, provide comfort measures per the coordinated care plan, and notify the hospice nurse of the symptom changes","Arrange immediate transfer to the emergency department","Wait for the family to arrive and ask them what they would like done"],"a":1},{"q":"Where should a resident's code status be verified?","c":["From the CNA who knows the resident best","From the POLST and physician orders in the resident's record","Assume full code for every resident to be safe","From the family's recollection at the time of the emergency"],"a":1},{"q":"Under the coordinated plan of care (F849), symptom medication adjustments for a hospice resident's terminal illness are primarily directed by:","c":["The hospice team in collaboration with the attending physician, with facility nurses implementing and communicating changes","The facility administrator","The CNA providing daily care","The pharmacy, acting independently"],"a":0}],"CNA":[{"q":"Frequent mouth and lip care helps keep a dying resident comfortable.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"If a dying resident stops eating, the CNA should keep pressing them to finish their meals.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"You should keep talking gently to a dying resident even if they seem unresponsive.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Roommates should be given the full medical details of a resident's death so they understand what happened.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"It is normal for staff to feel grief when a resident dies, and seeking support from a supervisor or facility resources is encouraged.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Grimacing, moaning, or restlessness in a dying resident should be reported to the nurse because they may be signs of pain.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Postmortem care may be done with the door and curtain open, since the resident has passed away.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"A family member is crying in the hallway after visiting their dying loved one. What should you do?","c":["Avoid them so you do not intrude on their grief","Acknowledge them kindly, offer a quiet place to sit, and let the nurse or Social Services know they may need support","Tell them not to cry because their loved one is comfortable","Share your own opinion about how much time their loved one has left"],"a":1},{"q":"You notice a hospice resident breathing with long pauses, with cool, bluish feet. What should you do?","c":["Begin CPR immediately","Tell the nurse right away, then stay nearby and continue comfort care as directed","Call 911 yourself from the unit phone","Nothing — hospice residents are cared for entirely by the hospice agency"],"a":1}]}},{"id":36,"key":"Fire_Prevention_Safety","month":10,"title":"Fire Prevention, Fire Extinguisher, Fire Alarm","guidelines":["Know the location of pull stations, extinguishers, fire/smoke barrier doors, oxygen zone valves, and evacuation routes on your assigned unit.","Keep corridors and exits clear; never prop open fire or smoke barrier doors; report electrical hazards immediately.","No smoking or open flames near oxygen in use or storage, per facility smoking policy.","Respond to any fire or alarm using RACE; fight only small, contained fires with your exit at your back, using PASS.","During an alarm: close doors, reassure and account for residents, move residents horizontally behind the nearest smoke barrier when directed, and await instructions from the person in charge.","Participate in all fire drills as if they were real events."],"roles":{"All Staff":[{"q":"The first step in RACE is to rescue anyone in immediate danger from the fire area.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"When the fire alarm sounds, staff should immediately begin evacuating all residents out of the building to the parking lot.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"In the PASS technique, you aim the extinguisher nozzle at the base of the fire, not at the flames.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"A corridor fire door may be propped open with a wedge during busy times, as long as staff are working nearby.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"Smoking and open flames are never allowed near oxygen that is in use or in storage.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Any staff member should shut off the oxygen zone valves as soon as the fire alarm sounds.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"You discover a small fire in a trash can in a resident's room, and the resident is in the room. What is your FIRST action?","c":["Run to get the nearest fire extinguisher","Remove the resident from immediate danger","Open the windows to let the smoke out","Go find the maintenance director"],"a":1},{"q":"\"Defend-in-place\" means:","c":["All residents are immediately evacuated to the parking lot","Staff remain in the fire area and fight the fire until the fire department arrives","Residents are protected behind closed doors and moved horizontally into the adjacent smoke compartment, with vertical or outside evacuation only if directed","Residents' doors are locked so smoke cannot enter their rooms"],"a":2}]}},{"id":37,"key":"LGBTQ_Resident_Care","month":10,"title":"LGBTQ+ Resident Care","guidelines":["Use the resident's chosen name and pronouns at all times, including in report and documentation practices per policy. If unsure, ask politely and privately.","Never disclose or discuss a resident's sexual orientation, gender identity, transition history, or HIV status except as needed for care with the resident's consent.","Support room assignments, clothing, grooming, and visitation consistent with the resident's identity and wishes, as required by SB 219.","If you make a mistake with a name or pronoun: correct yourself, apologize briefly, and move on.","Report any discrimination, mocking, or harassment of a resident to your supervisor, the DSD, or the Administrator. Good-faith reporting is protected from retaliation."],"roles":{"All Staff":[{"q":"Under SB 219, it is a violation to willfully and repeatedly refuse to use a resident's chosen name or pronouns after being clearly informed of them.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Staff may share a resident's sexual orientation or transgender status with curious coworkers, as long as no family members are told.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"Transgender residents have the right to a room assignment consistent with their gender identity, unless the resident requests otherwise.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"A resident's same-sex spouse may be restricted from visiting if other residents complain about the relationship.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"Residents have the right to wear clothing and grooming consistent with their gender identity.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"SB 219 protections apply only if the resident can prove they experienced discrimination before admission.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"You accidentally use the wrong pronoun for a resident. What is the BEST response?","c":["Ignore the mistake so you do not draw attention to it","Briefly apologize, correct yourself, and continue the conversation respectfully","Explain at length why the mistake was understandable","Avoid speaking with the resident so it does not happen again"],"a":1},{"q":"A coworker makes a joke about a resident's gender identity at the nurses' station. What should you do?","c":["Laugh along so things do not become awkward","Say nothing — it is not your business","Decline to join in and report the incident to your supervisor, the DSD, or the Administrator","Tell the resident what the coworker said"],"a":2}]}},{"id":38,"key":"Sensory_Special_Problems","month":10,"title":"Working with Special Problems (Blindness, Deafness, Confusion, Sensory Deprivation)","guidelines":["Announce yourself to residents with vision loss; use the sighted-guide technique for walking assistance and the clock method to describe food placement at meals.","Face hearing-impaired residents, gain attention first, reduce background noise, and speak clearly in a normal-to-lower pitch — never shout.","Care for adaptive devices: hearing aids stored in labeled cases (never on meal trays or in linens); glasses clean, in repair, and on the resident during waking hours. Report lost or broken devices the same day.","Sudden or fluctuating confusion is an acute change — report to the licensed nurse immediately; the nurse assesses and notifies the provider (F684).","Prevent sensory deprivation: devices in use, meaningful engagement during care, activity participation, and reporting of new withdrawal or disengagement."],"roles":{"Licensed Nurse":[{"q":"Sudden-onset confusion that fluctuates during the day is more consistent with delirium than dementia and requires provider notification.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"A resident with long-standing dementia who becomes newly lethargic and disoriented does not need provider notification, because confusion is expected in dementia.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"Shouting is the most effective way to communicate with a hearing-impaired resident.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"The clock method describes food placement on the plate (for example, \"meat at 6 o'clock\") so a resident with low vision can eat independently.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Hearing aids should be stored in a labeled container — never wrapped in a napkin or left on a meal tray.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Sensory deprivation can cause or worsen confusion, withdrawal, and depression.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Residents who are blind should be fed by staff at all meals to save time.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"A CNA reports that a resident who is normally alert and oriented is suddenly drowsy and confused this morning. The nurse's BEST first action is to:","c":["Chart the observation and recheck on the next shift","Assess the resident, obtain vital signs, and notify the provider of the acute change in condition","Assume it is a urinary tract infection and wait for routine labs","Reorient the resident and continue the normal routine"],"a":1},{"q":"Which of the following describes correct sighted-guide technique?","c":["The resident holds your arm just above the elbow and walks a half step behind you","You stand behind the resident and steer them by the shoulders","You hold the resident's arm and pull them forward beside you","The resident places a hand on your shoulder and you walk side by side without speaking"],"a":0},{"q":"Which intervention BEST prevents sensory deprivation in a room-bound resident with both vision and hearing loss?","c":["Keep the room quiet and dark so the resident can rest","Ensure glasses and hearing aids are on and working, and provide regular verbal and tactile engagement","Leave the television on at all times for continuous stimulation","Limit visitors so the resident is not overstimulated"],"a":1}],"CNA":[{"q":"You should always announce yourself when entering the room of a resident with vision loss.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"It is acceptable to leave a resident's hearing aids on the meal tray so they do not get misplaced.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"If a resident who is usually alert suddenly seems confused or very drowsy, you should tell the nurse right away.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Moving a blind resident's belongings to \"tidy up,\" without telling the resident, is helpful to them.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"Facing the resident and speaking clearly in a normal-to-lower tone helps a hearing-impaired resident understand you.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"A resident's glasses are only needed when the resident leaves their room.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"Describing food by clock position (\"meat at 6 o'clock\") helps a resident with low vision eat independently.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"You are walking a resident with severe vision loss to the activity room. What should you do?","c":["Push the resident gently ahead of you so they arrive faster","Offer your arm, let the resident hold just above your elbow, walk slightly ahead, and describe doorways and obstacles","Hold the resident's hand and pull them along beside you","Give the resident verbal directions and let them walk there alone"],"a":1},{"q":"A confused resident insists it is 1975 and says she must leave for work. What should you do?","c":["Correct her repeatedly until she accepts what year it is","Respond calmly without arguing, redirect her to an activity, and report the behavior to the nurse if it is new or worse","Close her door so she cannot wander while looking for work","Ask coworkers to ignore her so the behavior is not rewarded"],"a":1}]}},{"id":39,"key":"Abuse_Prevention_Elder_Justice","month":11,"title":"Abuse Prevention & Interventions — Elder Justice Act","guidelines":["Every employee is a mandated reporter; report a reasonable suspicion of abuse immediately — do not investigate first.","Apply Elder Justice Act §1150B timelines: within 2 hours if serious bodily injury, otherwise within 24 hours.","For resident-to-resident aggression, ensure safety, address injuries, notify the nurse/administration, and report per F609.","Staff who report in good faith are protected from retaliation; the facility investigates and protects residents per F610."],"roles":{"Licensed Nurse":[{"q":"Under the Elder Justice Act §1150B, staff must report a reasonable suspicion of a crime committed against a resident.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"If the suspected crime resulted in serious bodily injury, the report must be made within 2 hours; otherwise within 24 hours.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Only the Administrator has a duty to report suspected abuse; individual staff are exempt.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"Under W&I Code §15630, all facility staff are mandated reporters of suspected elder or dependent adult abuse.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Resident-to-resident aggression is never considered a reportable event.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"Staff who report suspected abuse in good faith are protected from retaliation.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"It is acceptable to complete the facility's internal investigation before reporting to the required agencies.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"A resident sustains a suspected physical assault resulting in a fracture (serious bodily injury). Under EJA §1150B, the report of reasonable suspicion of a crime must be made within:","c":["2 hours","24 hours","5 days","30 days"],"a":0},{"q":"Which of the following is an example of misappropriation of resident property (F602)?","c":["A staff member takes a resident's cash or belongings without consent","A resident gives a family member a gift","A resident misplaces their glasses","The facility inventories a resident's belongings on admission"],"a":0},{"q":"Which action reflects proper handling of a witnessed resident-to-resident altercation?","c":["Ignore it since no staff were involved","Separate and ensure safety, assess/report injuries, notify the nurse and administration, and report per requirements","Wait to see if it happens again before acting","Ask the residents to resolve it themselves"],"a":1}],"CNA":[{"q":"Every employee, including CNAs, has a duty to report suspected abuse.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Abuse can be physical, verbal, sexual, mental, or financial (misappropriation).","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"If you see something that looks like abuse, you should investigate it yourself before telling anyone.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"You should report suspected abuse immediately, even if you are not completely sure.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Reporting abuse in good faith can get you fired.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"Rough handling during care or ignoring call lights can be a form of abuse or neglect.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Taking a resident's belongings without permission is acceptable if you plan to return them later.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"You walk into a room and see a coworker roughly grabbing a resident and yelling. What should you do?","c":["Say nothing to avoid conflict","Ensure the resident's immediate safety and report to the nurse/supervisor right away","Confront and investigate the coworker yourself first","Wait until you clock out to mention it"],"a":1},{"q":"A resident tells you money is missing from their drawer. You should:","c":["Tell them they probably misplaced it","Report it to the nurse/supervisor and follow the facility's reporting process","Search other residents' rooms","Ignore it"],"a":1}]}},{"id":41,"key":"Bloodborne_Pathogens","month":11,"title":"Bloodborne Pathogens — HBV, HIV & PPE","guidelines":["Treat all blood and body fluids as potentially infectious and use standard precautions for every resident.","Never recap needles by hand; dispose of sharps immediately in a puncture-resistant, labeled container.","After any exposure, wash the area immediately and report to your supervisor at once for post-exposure evaluation.","The hepatitis B vaccine is offered at no cost to employees with occupational exposure risk."],"roles":{"All Staff":[{"q":"Bloodborne pathogens such as HBV, HCV, and HIV are spread through contact with blood and certain body fluids.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Standard precautions mean treating all blood and body fluids as if they are potentially infectious.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"It is safe to recap used needles by hand to save time.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"Cleaning up a small blood spill without gloves is acceptable as long as you wash your hands afterward.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"After a needlestick or blood exposure, you should finish your shift and report it the next day.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"The hepatitis B vaccine is offered at no cost to employees with occupational exposure risk.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Which is the correct FIRST step after a needlestick exposure?","c":["Wash the area immediately with soap and water","Keep working and monitor for symptoms","Squeeze the wound for several minutes then bandage it","Wait to see if you feel sick"],"a":0},{"q":"Which of the following is an example of correct standard precautions / PPE use?","c":["Wearing gloves when contact with blood or body fluids is anticipated","Reusing a single pair of gloves for every resident","Recapping needles by hand","Storing loose sharps in a pocket"],"a":0}]}},{"id":40,"key":"Behavioral_Health_Management","month":11,"title":"F740 / F741 — Behavioral Health & Management","guidelines":["Treat behavioral expressions as communication of an unmet need; identify and address the trigger before any pharmacological response.","Attempt and document person-centered, nonpharmacological interventions first; psychotropics require documented indication and monitoring.","Report any statement or sign of self-harm or suicidal ideation to the licensed nurse/provider immediately and ensure resident safety.","Document behaviors using antecedent, behavior, intervention, and response; licensed staff monitor gradual dose reduction unless contraindicated."],"roles":{"Licensed Nurse":[{"q":"Behavioral expressions such as agitation or calling out are often a form of communication signaling an unmet need.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Antipsychotic medications should always be the first-line response to a resident's agitation.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"Nonpharmacological interventions should be attempted and documented before considering psychotropic medication for behavioral symptoms.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"A resident stating they 'want to die' is a normal part of aging and does not require reporting.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"Gradual dose reduction (GDR) of psychotropic medications is attempted unless clinically contraindicated and documented.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Once a resident is started on a psychotropic medication, the dose can never be reduced.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"Documentation of a behavior should include the antecedent, the behavior itself, and the intervention and the resident's response.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Which is the BEST first response when a resident becomes agitated during care?","c":["Administer a PRN antipsychotic","Stop, step back, and try to identify the trigger or unmet need","Continue the task quickly to finish","Leave the resident alone in the room"],"a":1},{"q":"A resident with depression expresses hopelessness and a wish to not wake up. The nurse should:","c":["Document it and address it at the next care conference","Reassure the resident it will pass and move on","Take it seriously, ensure safety, and notify the provider promptly","Ignore it unless the resident repeats it three times"],"a":2},{"q":"Which requirement is reflected in F740/F741 for behavioral health?","c":["Every resident must be on a psychotropic medication","The facility must have sufficient staff with the competencies and skills to provide behavioral health services","Only the psychiatrist may address behaviors","Behavioral health services are optional"],"a":1}],"CNA":[{"q":"Behaviors like pacing or calling out can be a way a resident communicates a need such as pain, hunger, or needing to toilet.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"If a resident seems anxious or withdrawn, the CNA should report it to the licensed nurse.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"It is okay to argue with an upset resident to prove they are wrong.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"A calm voice, patience, and a familiar routine can help reduce a resident's distress.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"CNAs do not need to report a resident's comment about wanting to hurt themselves.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"Rushing a resident during care can trigger agitation.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Only the nurse ever notices changes in a resident's mood or behavior.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"A resident who is usually calm becomes agitated and tries to hit you during a transfer. What should you do FIRST?","c":["Restrain the resident's arms","Stop, keep yourself and the resident safe, use a calm voice, and report to the nurse","Argue with the resident","Finish the transfer as fast as possible"],"a":1},{"q":"A resident tells you, 'I don't want to be here anymore, I'd be better off dead.' You should:","c":["Keep it to yourself","Tell the resident to cheer up","Report it to the licensed nurse immediately","Wait until the end of your shift to mention it"],"a":2}]}},{"id":42,"key":"Theft_Loss_Grievance","month":11,"title":"Theft and Loss / Grievance Process","guidelines":["Inventory, mark, and safeguard residents' personal property; report lost or missing items promptly and document them.","Taking or 'borrowing' a resident's belongings without consent is misappropriation (F602) and must be reported.","Any staff member may receive a grievance; acknowledge it, route it to the grievance official/SSD, and never retaliate.","Residents have the right to a prompt response and written resolution of grievances under F585."],"roles":{"All Staff":[{"q":"Residents have the right to voice grievances without fear of retaliation.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Under F585, the facility must have a grievance official and provide a written response to grievances.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"'Borrowing' a resident's belongings without consent is acceptable as long as you intend to return them.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"Only the Administrator is allowed to receive a resident or family grievance.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"Residents' personal property should be inventoried on admission and lost items reported and documented.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"A resident complaint can be ignored as long as it seems minor.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"A family member tells you they are upset that their mother's hearing aid is missing. What is the BEST response?","c":["Tell them it is not your department","Acknowledge the concern, report it through the grievance/theft-and-loss process, and document it","Tell them to look for it themselves","Ignore it"],"a":1},{"q":"Which of the following reflects proper safeguarding of resident property?","c":["Inventorying and marking personal items and securing valuables","Leaving valuables unlabeled in open areas","Taking items home to keep them safe","Never documenting personal belongings"],"a":0}]}},{"id":43,"key":"Compliance_Ethics_Code_Of_Conduct","month":12,"title":"Compliance & Ethics / Code of Conduct P&P (F895 / F946)","guidelines":["The compliance and ethics program applies to every employee in every department — not just billing or management.","Never document care that was not provided, pre-chart, or sign for another person. Never clock in or out for a coworker.","Report suspected violations to your supervisor, the Administrator, or the compliance officer/hotline. Anonymous reporting is available.","Good-faith reporters are protected from retaliation. Retaliation is itself a violation and must be reported."],"roles":{"All Staff":[{"q":"Every employee — not just managers or billing staff — has a duty to report suspected compliance violations.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Documenting care before it is actually provided (“pre-charting”) is acceptable as long as you intend to provide the care later.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"You must give your name when reporting a compliance concern.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"Clocking in or out for a coworker who is running late is a form of timecard fraud.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"The compliance and ethics program only applies to the billing department.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"An employee who reports a concern in good faith is protected from retaliation.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Which of the following is an example of a compliance violation?","c":["Charting an assessment that was never performed","Reporting a concern to your supervisor","Asking the compliance officer a question about the Code of Conduct","Declining a vendor's gift and telling your supervisor about the offer"],"a":0},{"q":"You suspect a coworker is falsifying documentation. What should you do?","c":["Say nothing — it is not your responsibility","Confront the coworker and handle it between yourselves","Report it to your supervisor or through the compliance/anonymous reporting channel","Wait and mention it at the next annual survey"],"a":2}]}},{"id":44,"key":"QAPI_Five_Elements","month":12,"title":"QAPI — The Five Elements (F865 / F944)","guidelines":["QAPI covers all departments and all services — every staff member owns quality.","Report problems, near-misses, and improvement ideas in huddles, to your supervisor, or on rounding. Honest data is the fuel of QAPI.","A Performance Improvement Project (PIP) is a team effort to fix a problem area — it is never discipline.","When a problem occurs, the facility asks WHY it happened (root cause), not WHO to blame, and fixes the system so it cannot recur."],"roles":{"All Staff":[{"q":"QAPI applies to every department in the facility — not just nursing.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"The main purpose of QAPI is to identify and punish the staff member who caused a problem.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"Frontline staff such as CNAs, dietary aides, and housekeepers have a role in QAPI.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"A Performance Improvement Project (PIP) is a disciplinary action plan for an employee.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"QAPI decisions should be based on data, not just opinions.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Only the QAPI committee is responsible for quality of care in the facility.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"How many elements make up a QAPI program?","c":["Three","Four","Five","Ten"],"a":2},{"q":"You notice the same type of problem happening over and over on your unit. What is the best QAPI-minded response?","c":["Quietly work around it — that's just how things are","Report it so it can be tracked, analyzed, and fixed at the system level","Figure out which shift is causing it and blame them","Wait to see if anyone else notices it first"],"a":1}]}},{"id":45,"key":"Oxygen_Delivery_Devices","month":8,"title":"Oxygen Delivery Devices — Nasal Cannula, OxyMask & Non-Rebreather Mask (F695)","guidelines":["Oxygen is a medication. The device and flow rate in use must always match the current physician order.","Nasal cannula: 1–6 L/min (approx. 24–44% FiO2). Never above 6 L/min. Check skin behind ears, cheeks, and nares every shift.","OxyMask: 1–15 L/min (approx. 24–90% FiO2). Open design vents CO2 — no minimum flow. Resident can talk and drink with a straw.","Non-rebreather: 10–15 L/min ONLY. Inflate the reservoir bag before applying; never run below 10 L/min. Emergency device — notify the physician/NP immediately and monitor frequently.","Safety every shift: no smoking or open flames near oxygen, cylinders secured upright, tubing free of kinks and trip hazards, concentrator plugged directly into the wall.","CNAs never change the flow rate or remove oxygen — report dislodged devices, low tanks, skin redness, and any breathing changes to the licensed nurse immediately."],"roles":{"Licensed Nurse":[{"q":"T / F — Oxygen is a medication and requires a physician order specifying flow rate and delivery device.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — A nasal cannula may be set as high as 10 L/min if the resident's saturation is low.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — The OxyMask's open design vents exhaled CO2, so it can safely be run at flows as low as 1 L/min.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — A non-rebreather mask may be run at 6 L/min as long as the reservoir bag looks inflated.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — The reservoir bag of a non-rebreather must be fully inflated before the mask is applied to the resident.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — A resident placed on a non-rebreather requires immediate physician/NP notification and evaluation for a higher level of care.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — For a resident with COPD who retains CO2, oxygen should be titrated to the ordered target saturation rather than the highest possible SpO2.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"A resident on a nasal cannula at 4 L/min has an SpO2 of 84% and increasing shortness of breath. After increasing oxygen per protocol, the resident’s saturation only responds when placed on a non-rebreather. The nurse’s flow rate setting should be:","c":["5 L/min","8 L/min","10–15 L/min, keeping the reservoir bag inflated","2 L/min to avoid oxygen toxicity"],"a":2},{"q":"Which device allows a resident to talk clearly and drink with a straw while receiving oxygen at flows from 1–15 L/min?","c":["Nasal cannula","OxyMask","Non-rebreather mask","Simple face mask"],"a":1},{"q":"During rounds you find a resident wearing a nasal cannula at 5 L/min, but the order reads “O2 at 2 L/min via nasal cannula, titrate to SpO2 ≥ 92%.” The BEST action is:","c":["Leave it — more oxygen is always safer","Assess the resident, check SpO2, adjust per the titration order, and document the finding and response","Ask the CNA to turn it down when they have time","Discontinue the oxygen entirely"],"a":1}],"CNA":[{"q":"T / F — If a resident's oxygen saturation alarm sounds, the CNA should turn up the oxygen flow rate.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — Oxygen is a medication — only the licensed nurse may change the flow rate or remove it.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — Redness on the skin behind a resident's ears from oxygen tubing should be reported to the nurse.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — It is okay for a resident to smoke as long as their oxygen is turned off for a few minutes.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — A resident wearing an OxyMask can drink with a straw without removing the mask.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — Oxygen cylinders (tanks) must be secured upright in a rack or carrier — never left free-standing.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — If the bag on a resident's non-rebreather mask looks flat or deflated, the CNA should tell the nurse immediately.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"You find a resident's nasal cannula lying on the floor and the resident looks short of breath. What should you do FIRST?","c":["Put the cannula back on, turn up the flow, and continue your rounds","Stay with the resident and call the licensed nurse immediately","Throw the cannula away and get a new one after lunch","Turn off the oxygen concentrator"],"a":1},{"q":"Which of the following should a CNA report to the nurse right away?","c":["A kinked oxygen tube caught under a wheelchair wheel","A resident who is breathing faster than usual or whose lips look bluish","An oxygen tank gauge that is reading low","All of the above"],"a":3}]}},{"id":46,"key":"Purposeful_Rounding_6Ps","month":8,"title":"Purposeful Rounding — The 6 P’s (F689)","guidelines":["The 6 P’s of purposeful rounding are Pain, Potty, Position, Possessions, Peaceful environment, and Promise — address all six at every round instead of waiting for call lights.","Most falls occur during unassisted toileting attempts. Proactively offering toileting help on a schedule is the single most effective fall-prevention step.","Report immediately to the licensed nurse: new pain, new confusion or change in condition, skin changes, repeated refusals, or unsafe equipment.","If the resident is asleep, do not wake them — quietly verify the call light is in reach, the bed is low with brakes locked, and the pathway is clear.","Close every round with “Is there anything else I can do for you? I have time,” and tell the resident when someone will be back. Document every round."],"roles":{"Licensed Nurse":[{"q":"T / F — Purposeful rounding means checking on every resident on a scheduled basis and proactively addressing the 6 P’s, rather than waiting for call lights.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — Most resident falls in a skilled nursing facility occur when residents attempt to toilet themselves without assistance.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — If a resident is asleep during rounding, you should wake them so all 6 P’s can be completed.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — A CNA’s report of new or worsening pain during rounding requires nursing assessment and intervention per orders — not just documentation at end of shift.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — Rounding is complete once the room has been entered; documentation of the round is optional.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — Repositioning residents during rounds supports pressure injury prevention for at-risk residents.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — The “Promise” step — telling the resident when staff will return — tends to increase call-light use because it reminds residents to call.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"During rounding you find a high-fall-risk resident starting to get up to use the bathroom alone. The BEST response is:","c":["Remind the resident to use the call light and continue your rounds","Assist the resident with toileting now, per the care plan","Tell the resident to wait until the scheduled toileting time","Ask a family member to help the resident"],"a":1},{"q":"Which finding during rounding requires immediate nursing follow-up rather than routine documentation?","c":["The resident’s water pitcher is empty","The resident asks for an extra blanket","The resident has new confusion and slurred speech","The television remote is out of reach"],"a":2},{"q":"Which of the following correctly lists the 6 P’s of purposeful rounding?","c":["Pain, Potty, Position, Possessions, Peaceful environment, Promise","Pain, Pulse, Position, Posture, Privacy, Paperwork","Potty, Pills, Position, Possessions, Privacy, Promise","Pain, Potty, Posture, Pills, Peaceful environment, Paperwork"],"a":0}],"CNA":[{"q":"T / F — Offering toileting help on a schedule is one of the most effective ways to prevent falls.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — If a resident refuses help during your round, you should not offer again for the rest of the shift.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — If a resident reports new pain during rounding, the CNA should wait and mention it at the end of shift.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — Before leaving the room, the call light, water, glasses, and phone should be within the resident’s reach.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — Rounding only needs to be done for residents who use their call light frequently.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — The bed should be in the lowest position with brakes locked before you leave the room.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — Before leaving, you should ask “Is there anything else I can do for you? I have time,” and tell the resident when someone will be back.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"During your round, a resident who is a one-person assist with a gait belt says they need to use the bathroom. What should you do?","c":["Tell them to use the call light when it becomes urgent","Assist them to the bathroom now, using the gait belt","Ask them to wait for the licensed nurse","Set them up on the bedside commode and leave the room"],"a":1},{"q":"Which of the following should you check before leaving the resident’s room?","c":["Call light within reach","Bed in lowest position with brakes locked","Pathway clear and personal items within reach","All of the above"],"a":3}]}},{"id":47,"key":"Veras_Availability_PRN","month":8,"title":"Entering Your Availability in Veras (Per-Diem / On-Call Staff)","guidelines":["Availability for the upcoming month must be entered in the Veras mobile app before the 1st of that month.","A date left blank in Veras is presumed available — you are only marking the exceptions.","“Unavailable” means “do not schedule me.” “Preferred” means “I can work, and these are my best hours.”","Nothing saves until you tap the purple “Add Preference” button at the bottom of the form.","Active per-diem status requires at least one shift per month, one weekend shift per month, one holiday shift per year, and a response to shift offers within four hours during waking hours."],"roles":{"Licensed Nurse":[{"q":"T / F — Availability for the upcoming month must be submitted in Veras before the 1st of that month.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — If you leave a date blank in Veras, the facility assumes you are unavailable that day.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — Selecting “Preferred” on a date tells the scheduler you cannot work that day.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — Tapping “+ Add Preference” saves your entry immediately.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — Per-diem staff must be available for at least one weekend shift per month.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — You are expected to respond to an individual shift offer within four hours during waking hours.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — Submitting a time-off request is the correct way to set your monthly availability.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"In the Veras mobile app, where do you go to set your availability?","c":["Home → Messages","Schedule → the three dots in the upper right → Manage Schedule Preferences","Time → Punch history","Open Shifts → Request"],"a":1},{"q":"You cannot work September 12. Which action correctly blocks that date?","c":["Tap Sept 12, choose Preferred, set 7:00 AM – 7:00 PM","Tap Sept 12, choose Unavailable, set times covering the day, then tap the purple Add Preference button","Leave Sept 12 blank so the scheduler skips it","Send the ADON a text message and change nothing in Veras"],"a":1},{"q":"Failing to meet the minimum activity requirements for how long may result in inactive status?","c":["One month","Two consecutive months","Three consecutive months","Six consecutive months"],"a":1}],"CNA":[{"q":"T / F — You must update your availability in Veras before the 1st of each month.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — If you leave a day blank in Veras, the facility assumes you cannot work that day.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — “Unavailable” means “please do not schedule me.”","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — You should choose “Preferred” for a day you cannot work.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — Your entry is saved as soon as you tap “+ Add Preference” the first time.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — Per-diem staff need to be available for at least one weekend shift each month.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — If you cannot work a shift you already accepted, you must notify the facility at least four hours before it starts.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Maria is per-diem and has class every Tuesday in September. What should she do?","c":["Nothing — the scheduler already knows her class schedule","Before September 1, mark each Tuesday Unavailable in Veras (or use the Repeat switch)","Mark each Tuesday “Preferred” so the office knows she is busy","Wait until she is offered a Tuesday shift, then decline it"],"a":1},{"q":"James opens Veras, taps a date, taps “+ Add Preference,” selects Unavailable, then closes the app. He is scheduled that day anyway. Why?","c":["Veras was down that day","He never tapped the purple “Add Preference” button, so nothing saved","Unavailable only works for weekend dates","The ADON overrode his request"],"a":1}]}},{"id":48,"key":"Stop_and_Watch","month":9,"title":"Stop and Watch Early Warning Tool","guidelines":["Stop and Watch is the early warning tool CNAs and other non-licensed staff use to report subtle changes in a resident's condition to the licensed nurse (Jericho Change in Condition P&P).","Compare what you see to the resident's usual baseline. STOP AND WATCH: Seems different, Talks less, Overall needs more help, Pain, Ate less, No BM/diarrhea, Drank less, Weight change, Agitated, Tired/confused, Change in skin, Help needed more.","Tell the licensed nurse right away, out loud — then complete the Stop and Watch documentation. The form is the record that you reported it; it never replaces telling the nurse.","Emergencies (not breathing, chest pain, unresponsive, stroke signs, fall with injury) are not a form: stay with the resident and call for the nurse immediately.","Licensed nurses assess promptly, organize findings with SBAR, notify the physician by urgency, notify the representative within 24 hours (except emergencies), document, and close the loop with the CNA."],"roles":{"Licensed Nurse":[{"q":"T / F — Stop and Watch is an early warning tool used by CNAs and other non-licensed staff to communicate subtle changes in a resident's condition to the licensed nurse.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — When a CNA reports a Stop and Watch concern, the nurse may wait until the end of the shift to look at the resident if the resident appears comfortable.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — Except in medical emergencies, the resident's representative must be notified of a significant change in condition within 24 hours.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — Before calling the physician about a change in condition, the nurse should assess the resident and organize the findings using the SBAR format.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — A weight change of 5 pounds or more within 30 days does not require physician notification as long as the resident is eating well.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — If the attending physician does not respond in a timely manner, the nurse should document the attempt and wait until the next business day.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — A Stop and Watch report from a CNA can take the place of a nursing assessment.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"A CNA tells you a resident \"seems different\" — sleepier than usual and ate less than half of breakfast. Your FIRST action should be:","c":["Note it on the 24-hour report for the next shift","Assess the resident now, including vital signs, level of consciousness, and a focused assessment","Call the resident's family","Ask the CNA to keep watching and report back at the end of the shift"],"a":1},{"q":"A resident has new-onset confusion and a fever. Under the Change in Condition policy, this is:","c":["Routine — notify the practitioner within 24 hours","Urgent — call the physician and request a prompt response","Not a change that requires physician notification","Emergent — call 911 for every resident with a fever"],"a":1},{"q":"After a significant change in condition, who must be notified in addition to the physician and the resident's representative?","c":["The Director of Nursing and the MDS Coordinator","The business office only","The Ombudsman, for every change in condition","No one else needs to be notified"],"a":0}],"CNA":[{"q":"T / F — The Stop and Watch tool helps you report small changes you notice in a resident before they become serious.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — If you complete a Stop and Watch form, you do not need to tell the nurse out loud.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — The \"P\" in STOP AND WATCH stands for new or worsening pain.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — A resident drinking less than usual is a normal part of aging and does not need to be reported.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — Needing more help than usual with walking, transferring, or toileting is a change that should be reported.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — If the nurse is busy, it is OK to wait until the end of your shift to report a change.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — Completing Stop and Watch documentation creates a record that you told the nurse, so the change gets follow-up.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Resident A usually chats with you during morning care. Today Resident A is quiet, ate only a few bites of breakfast, and seems more tired than usual. What should you do?","c":["Let Resident A rest and check again at the end of your shift","Tell the licensed nurse right away and complete the Stop and Watch documentation","Mention it to the next shift during report","Only record the meal intake in POC"],"a":1},{"q":"While you are helping a resident to the bathroom, the resident suddenly cannot speak clearly and one side of the face is drooping. What should you do?","c":["Complete a Stop and Watch form and give it to the nurse at your next break","Stay with the resident and call for the nurse immediately — this is an emergency","Finish toileting, put the resident to bed, and then report it","Wait a few minutes to see if it goes away"],"a":1}]}},{"id":49,"key":"Pending_Diagnostic_Order","month":9,"title":"Pending Diagnostic Order — Nurse-to-CNA Communication and Activity Precautions (F658)","guidelines":["When a study (X-ray, CT, ultrasound, lab) is ordered to rule out a fracture or other acute injury — usually after a fall or new pain — the resident is on activity precautions until the result is back, the provider is notified, and new orders are given and passed along (SWI-Nursing-Pending-Diagnostic-Communication).","The nurse who obtains the order also obtains the provider's activity, transfer, and weight-bearing orders in the same call. If the provider does not specify, ASK. Default until told otherwise: bedrest — no transfer out of bed except for medically necessary care, using the safest method and the least movement of the affected limb.","Document before you leave: complete the INTERACT SBAR — the change, your assessment, pain score, interventions offered, any refusal, provider notification, orders received, representative notification — before the end of the shift. A late entry on the next shift is not acceptable.","Flag the resident in two places, in plain language a CNA can act on: the CNA assignment sheet and the nurse alert charting report. \"NO transfer out of bed — pending hip X-ray,\" not \"see chart.\"","Tell every assigned CNA face to face and ask them to repeat it back. Hand off the pending study and precautions at shift report; the oncoming nurse re-briefs their own CNAs at the start of the shift.","CNAs: before moving anyone who fell, check the assignment sheet. If you are not sure whether a study is pending, ask the nurse before you transfer. New pain during a transfer means STOP, return the resident to a safe position, and tell the nurse immediately.","When the result comes back: read it, notify the provider, document the time received and the time notified, obtain new orders, notify the representative. Precautions stay in force until the result is reviewed, the provider is notified, new orders are documented and told to the CNAs face to face, and the care plan is updated. Only a licensed nurse with a provider order lifts a precaution."],"roles":{"Licensed Nurse":[{"q":"T / F — When a diagnostic study is ordered to rule out a fracture, the nurse obtains the provider's activity, transfer, and weight-bearing orders in the same call.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — If the provider does not specify activity orders, the resident may be mobilized as usual until the result is back.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — The INTERACT SBAR for a change in condition may be completed as a late entry on the next shift if the nurse stayed late to enter the order.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — The pending study and activity precautions are recorded on both the CNA assignment sheet and the nurse alert charting report.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — A note in the chart is an acceptable substitute for telling the assigned CNA about the precaution in person.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — The oncoming nurse must re-brief their own CNAs on the pending study and precautions at the start of the shift.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — A result received on night shift may be held for day shift to notify the provider.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"A resident who fell last night complains of new hip pain and cannot turn during care. You assess the resident and call the provider, who orders a hip X-ray. Before the call ends, what must you also obtain?","c":["A pain medication order only","Activity, transfer, and weight-bearing orders to hold until the result is back","A dietary consult","Permission to get the resident up to the wheelchair for meals"],"a":1},{"q":"Which of the following lifts an activity precaution?","c":["The CNA reports that the resident says they feel fine","The X-ray technician finishes the study","A licensed nurse, with a provider order, after the result is received, the provider is notified, and new orders are documented and told to the CNAs","The end of the shift in which the order was written"],"a":2},{"q":"Which of the following must be documented before the end of the shift in which the change in condition was identified?","c":["The change, your assessment and pain score, interventions offered and any refusal, provider and representative notification, and orders received","Only the provider's order for the study","Only the vital signs","Nothing until the result returns"],"a":0}],"CNA":[{"q":"T / F — Before moving a resident who fell, check the assignment sheet for activity precautions.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — If you are not sure whether an X-ray is pending, it is OK to transfer the resident as long as you use a gait belt.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — \"NO transfer out of bed — pending hip X-ray\" on the assignment sheet means the resident stays in bed except for medically necessary care.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — If a resident complains of new pain during a transfer, finish the transfer quickly and then tell the nurse.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — When the nurse tells you about a precaution, repeating it back helps make sure you both understand it.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — A resident's activity precaution ends when the X-ray technician leaves.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — You will never be criticized for stopping to ask the nurse before you transfer a resident.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"You are assigned to a resident who fell last night. The assignment sheet says nothing about precautions, but the resident winces and says the hip hurts when you help them sit up. What should you do?","c":["Continue the transfer to the wheelchair, then mention it at break","Stop, return the resident to a safe position, and tell the nurse immediately","Give the resident a pillow and try again later","Ask the resident if they would rather skip breakfast"],"a":1},{"q":"At the start of your shift, the nurse tells you Resident B has a pending pelvic X-ray and is on bedrest. Which is correct?","c":["Repeat the precaution back to the nurse and keep Resident B in bed except for medically necessary care","Get Resident B up to the wheelchair for lunch because they usually eat in the dining room","Wait for the CNA from the previous shift to tell you what to do","Ask therapy to walk Resident B to see if the hip still hurts"],"a":0}]}},{"id":50,"key":"Resident_Positioning_Person_Centered_Care","month":10,"title":"Resident Positioning and Person-Centered Care — In Bed and in the Wheelchair (F686/F676)","guidelines":["Positioning is individualized, not one-size-fits-all. Before you position anyone, check the care plan and the CNA assignment sheet: turning schedule, head-of-bed limits, weight-bearing status, hip or spine precautions, splints, number of staff and lift required, cushion type, and the resident's own preferences (which side, how high, which pillows).","Person-centered means the resident leads. Explain each step, ask what is comfortable, and honor preferences and routines. A resident may decline a position change — offer an alternative, make them as safe and comfortable as they will allow, and tell the nurse so the care plan can be updated. Never force a position.","In bed: keep the body in alignment (head neutral, spine straight, hips not twisted, feet supported and not pressed against the footboard). Use the 30-degree lateral tilt supported by pillows or a wedge rather than a full 90-degree side-lying position. Float the heels. Keep the head of the bed at the lowest elevation the resident's condition allows (30 degrees or less when possible) to limit shear, except during and for 30–60 minutes after meals or tube feeding, when the head of bed is at 30–45 degrees or upright as ordered.","Reposition bed-bound and at-risk residents at least every 2 hours, or more often if the care plan says so. Never position a resident on an existing pressure injury or on a reddened area. Use a lift sheet or repositioning aid with two staff — never drag. Check the skin over every bony prominence at each turn.","In the wheelchair: hips all the way back in the seat, hips/knees/ankles at about 90 degrees, pelvis level, trunk upright, head midline, arms supported, feet on the footrests or flat on the floor — never dangling. The chair must fit the resident and fit under the dining table. Foot pedals come off for a resident who self-propels with the feet and go on for one who does not.","Seated residents need weight shifts at least every hour (a resident who can do it alone is taught to shift every 15 minutes) and a return to bed or a position change within the sitting-time limit in the care plan. A resident who has slid down into a sacral-sitting posture is repositioned with two staff and a gait belt or a sit-to-stand approach — never pulled up by the arms or under the armpits.","Positioning devices (wedges, lateral supports, pommel cushions, seat belts, lap trays, tilt-in-space) are postural supports only when the resident can release them easily or they are used for alignment and the resident is free to move. If a device keeps the resident from moving freely or getting out of the chair when they want to, it is a restraint and requires a physician's order, informed consent, assessment, and the least restrictive option (F604; Title 22 §72319). Devices are never used for staff convenience.","Report and document: new redness, blisters, skin tears, pain with positioning, sliding down, poorly fitting equipment, refusals, and anything the resident asks for. CNAs record each turn on the turning/repositioning record and the flow sheet. Licensed nurses assess, update the care plan, notify the provider and representative for any new pressure injury, and request an OT/PT seating evaluation when the chair does not fit."],"roles":{"Licensed Nurse":[{"q":"T / F — A single facility-wide turning schedule (every 2 hours for everyone) satisfies the requirement for an individualized repositioning plan.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — A 30-degree lateral tilt supported by pillows or a wedge is preferred over a full 90-degree side-lying position for pressure redistribution.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — A resident who refuses to be turned may simply be left in the same position and the refusal does not need to be reported or care planned.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — Keeping the head of the bed elevated above 30 degrees for long periods increases shear and pressure over the sacrum and coccyx.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — A wheelchair seat belt that the resident cannot release on their own is a physical restraint and requires a physician's order, informed consent, and assessment.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — A resident receiving continuous tube feeding should have the head of the bed at 30 to 45 degrees during the feeding and for 30 to 60 minutes after it stops.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — If a resident's wheelchair is too wide or too deep, it is acceptable to pad the seat with pillows rather than request a seating evaluation.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"During rounds you find a resident slumped in the wheelchair with the buttocks forward on the seat and the feet off the footrests. The care plan says the resident may sit up to 2 hours and uses a gel cushion. What is the BEST response?","c":["Have one CNA pull the resident up under the arms so the resident is sitting straight","Have two CNAs reposition the resident using a gait belt or sit-to-stand method, check the cushion is correct side up, place the feet on the footrests, confirm time up, and assess the skin if the limit is near","Add a seat belt so the resident stops sliding","Return the resident to bed and discontinue chair time"],"a":1},{"q":"Which of the following belongs in a resident's individualized positioning plan in the care plan?","c":["Turning frequency, preferred positions and sides, head-of-bed limits, devices and cushions, number of staff and lift, sitting-time limit, and the resident's stated preferences","Only the turning frequency","Only the support surface","The CNA's preferred routine for that hallway"],"a":0},{"q":"A CNA reports new non-blanchable redness on a resident's right hip after a 2-hour side-lying period. Which statement is correct?","c":["Keep the current schedule; redness after a 2-hour turn is expected","Position the resident on the right side again to see if the redness blanches next time","Assess and document the finding, keep the resident off the right hip, shorten the interval, notify the provider and representative, and revise the care plan","Apply a donut cushion over the hip"],"a":2}],"CNA":[{"q":"T / F — Before positioning a resident, check the care plan or CNA assignment sheet for turning schedule, head-of-bed limits, precautions, and the resident's preferences.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — When a resident is lying on their side, turning them a full 90 degrees onto the hip bone is the best way to relieve pressure on the tailbone.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — A resident's feet should rest on the footrests or flat on the floor — never dangle — while sitting in the wheelchair.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — If a resident says they do not want to be turned right now, you should turn them anyway because the schedule requires it.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — A resident who can shift their own weight in the wheelchair should be reminded to do so about every 15 minutes.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — It is OK to put a lap tray or seat belt on a resident who keeps trying to stand up, as long as it is only for a short time.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — After a meal, a resident who eats in bed should stay sitting upright for at least 30 minutes before being laid flat.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Resident A has been in the wheelchair in the day room for about 2 hours and has slid down so the hips are forward and the tailbone is on the seat. Resident A cannot shift alone. What should you do?","c":["Pull Resident A up by the arms from behind so the back is straight","Get a second staff member, reposition Resident A with a gait belt or sit-to-stand technique so the hips are all the way back, check that the cushion is right side up and the feet are on the footrests, and tell the nurse the 2-hour limit has been reached","Tilt the chair back and leave Resident A to rest","Add a pillow under the hips so the resident sits taller"],"a":1},{"q":"Resident B's care plan says turn every 2 hours, prefers lying on the left side facing the window, head of bed no higher than 30 degrees, and float heels. When you turn Resident B to the left you see a red area on the left hip that does not fade when you press it. What should you do?","c":["Finish positioning Resident B on the left side as the care plan says and chart the redness at the end of the shift","Rub the red area to bring back circulation and then position Resident B on it","Keep Resident B off the left hip, use a 30-degree tilt or another position Resident B accepts, float the heels, and tell the nurse right away","Raise the head of the bed to 60 degrees so Resident B stays off the hip"],"a":2}]}},{"id":51,"key":"Transfer_Discharge_Bed_Hold","month":10,"title":"Transfer, Discharge & Readmission — Notice, Bed-Hold and Return Rights (F627 / F628)","guidelines":["A facility-initiated transfer or discharge is allowed only for the six reasons in 42 CFR §483.15(c)(1) and Title 22 §72527(a)(6): the resident's needs cannot be met here, the resident has improved and no longer needs our services, safety of others, health of others, non-payment after proper notice, or facility closure. Never for a change in payer source (AFL 23-37).","Written notice goes to the resident AND the representative in a language and manner they understand, with a copy to the LTC Ombudsman, at least 30 days before a facility-initiated transfer or discharge — or as soon as practicable for the health/safety, improvement, urgent-medical-need, or under-30-day-stay exceptions (AFL 25-17).","The notice must contain: the specific reason and its legal basis, effective date, the destination, appeal rights with the DHCS Office of Administrative Hearings and Appeals contact information and how to get the form, and the Ombudsman's name/address/phone. Add the Disability Rights California contact for residents with ID/DD or mental illness.","Every resident transferred to a hospital or on therapeutic leave gets a WRITTEN bed-hold notice at the time of transfer: 7-day bed-hold option, the resident/representative must tell us within 24 hours if they want it, and non-Medi-Cal residents may be charged for bed-hold days (Title 22 §72520; AFL 25-24).","A resident who returns within the 7-day bed hold returns to the same bed. After the bed hold expires, a resident who still needs our services is readmitted to the first available appropriate bed — a Medi-Cal resident even with an outstanding balance. A resident who appeals a discharge stays (or returns) while the appeal is pending.","An emergency transfer (including a 5150 hold) is NOT a discharge. Document the reasons in the medical record, send the Ombudsman copy, and plan for the resident's return."],"roles":{"Licensed Nurse":[{"q":"T / F — A facility may discharge a resident because the resident's Medicare days have ended and the resident is converting to Medi-Cal.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — An emergency transfer to the hospital, including a 5150 psychiatric hold, is the same as a discharge from the facility.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — The written bed-hold notice must be given to the resident or representative at the time of transfer to the hospital, not only on admission.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — If a resident wants to exercise the bed hold, the resident or representative must notify the facility within 24 hours after being informed of the right.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — A resident whose 7-day bed hold has expired, and who still needs skilled nursing care, must be readmitted to the first available bed in a semi-private room.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — A Medi-Cal resident may be refused readmission after hospitalization if there is an outstanding Medi-Cal balance.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — Once a discharge notice has been given, the resident may be discharged on the effective date even if an appeal has been filed and is still pending.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"Which of the following is a permitted reason for a facility-initiated discharge under 42 CFR §483.15(c)(1)?","c":["The resident's family has been difficult to work with","The resident's needs cannot be met in the facility and the physician has documented this in the medical record","A referral with better insurance is waiting for the bed","The resident is a short-term rehab admission who says they are not ready to go home"],"a":1},{"q":"A resident is being sent to the emergency room tonight for chest pain. Before the ambulance leaves, the nurse must:","c":["Complete a 30-day discharge notice and send it to the Ombudsman","Give the resident/representative the written bed-hold notice, send the POLST and transfer packet, and document the transfer and reasons in the record","Nothing — emergency transfers have no notice requirement","Call the DHCS Office of Administrative Hearings and Appeals"],"a":1},{"q":"What is the standard advance notice period for a facility-initiated transfer or discharge, and which exceptions allow a shorter notice?","c":["72 hours; no exceptions","14 days; shorter if the resident agrees","30 days; shorter (as soon as practicable) only for safety/health of others, sufficient improvement, urgent medical need, or a stay under 30 days","30 days; shorter whenever the Administrator approves"],"a":2}],"All Staff":[{"q":"T / F — A facility-initiated discharge notice must be given to the resident and the resident's representative in writing, in a language and manner they understand.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — A copy of every facility-initiated transfer or discharge notice must be sent to the Long-Term Care Ombudsman.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — The discharge notice only needs to state the date of discharge; the reason can be explained verbally.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — A resident who was admitted for short-term rehab can be discharged when the rehab episode ends, even if the resident says they are not ready to leave.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — The facility may not ask a resident or family to promise they will not apply for Medi-Cal as a condition of admission or staying.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — A resident returning from the hospital within the 7-day bed hold may be moved to a different room if a new admission has been placed in their bed.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"A family member calls and says the hospital told them their mother will not be coming back to Morning Star because \"her Medicare ran out.\" The resident still needs skilled nursing care. What should you do?","c":["Tell the family the hospital is correct and refer them to another facility","Explain that a change in payment source is not a reason for discharge, that the resident has bed-hold and return rights, and route the call to the Administrator or Social Services Director the same day","Ask the family to pay privately before she can return","Wait for the hospital case manager to send paperwork"],"a":1},{"q":"The Business Office prepares a 30-day discharge notice for non-payment. Before it is delivered, which of the following must be true?","c":["The Administrator has approved it, the resident has received reasonable and appropriate notice of the balance, the notice contains all required elements including appeal and Ombudsman contact, and a copy goes to the Ombudsman","The resident has been told verbally that they owe money","The bed has already been promised to a new admission","The physician has written a discharge order"],"a":0}]}},{"id":52,"key":"Zoll_LifeVest","month":10,"title":"Caring for a Resident with a ZOLL LifeVest Wearable Defibrillator (F684/F678)","guidelines":["The LifeVest is a wearable cardioverter-defibrillator (WCD) prescribed for residents at temporary high risk of sudden cardiac arrest. It watches the heart rhythm continuously and, if it detects ventricular tachycardia or ventricular fibrillation, delivers a treatment shock on its own — usually 25 to 60 seconds after detection, up to 5 shocks per event. It does NOT treat a slow heart rate or asystole, and it does NOT call 911 or notify anyone.","It only protects the resident when it is on. Wear is 24 hours a day, including sleep. It comes off only for a brief shower or bath, with staff nearby, and goes back on right away. Any refusal to wear it is reported to the provider, documented, and care planned.","Daily care: swap the battery once a day (the resident feels a vibration on the back when it is changed) and put the spare on the charger. Wash the garment every 1–2 days with no bleach, bleach alternatives, fabric softener, or anti-static products. Never wash the electrode belt or the monitor. Check the skin under the electrodes and straps every shift.","Alerts: a vibration or a gong (single tone) means read the monitor screen and follow the message. A two-tone siren means the device has detected a dangerous rhythm and is preparing to treat.","ONLY THE RESIDENT presses the response buttons. Holding them is the device's consciousness test — a conscious resident can delay a shock. If staff press them, the device may withhold a shock an unconscious resident needs.","When the siren sounds or the voice says \"Press response buttons to delay treatment\" or \"Bystanders, do not interfere\": do NOT touch the resident and do NOT start CPR — call for help, bring the crash cart/AED, and let the device treat. Begin CPR (if the resident is a full code) when the device says \"If patient is not responsive, call for help. Perform CPR.\" or the siren stops.","Blue gel on the chest and back means the device has released gel for a shock. Leave it on while the vest is worn. To use the facility AED, disconnect the monitor from the electrode belt first (the garment and belt can stay on). To remove the vest, pull the battery out first, then take off the garment. Send the monitor, charger, and spare battery with the resident to the hospital.","After any treatment or alarm event: assess, notify the provider and representative immediately (NS-CIC-001), call ZOLL 24/7 at 1-800-543-3267, and document. A resident with a DNR/POLST \"No CPR\" order who is wearing a LifeVest is a conflict — notify the provider right away to reconcile; never remove the vest without an order."],"roles":{"Licensed Nurse":[{"q":"T / F — When the LifeVest siren is sounding and the device says \"Bystanders, do not interfere,\" the nurse should immediately start chest compressions.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — Only the resident should ever press the LifeVest response buttons.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — A resident wearing a LifeVest may take it off at night so they can sleep more comfortably.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — If blue gel is found on the resident's chest and back while the LifeVest is still being worn, the nurse should wipe the gel off right away.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — Before delivering a shock with the facility AED, the LifeVest monitor should be disconnected from the electrode belt.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — When the LifeVest delivers a shock, it automatically notifies 911 and the resident's cardiologist.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — The LifeVest battery should be changed once a day and the spare battery placed on the charger.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"Resident A is awake and talking when the LifeVest begins to vibrate, a siren sounds, and the voice says \"Press response buttons to delay treatment.\" Resident A says, \"I feel fine.\" What is the BEST action?","c":["Press and hold the response buttons for Resident A so the device does not shock","Coach Resident A to press and hold the response buttons, call for help and the crash cart/AED, stay with Resident A, assess vital signs and level of consciousness, and notify the provider","Pull the battery out of the monitor to stop the alarm","Tell Resident A to ignore the alarm because it is probably a false alert"],"a":1},{"q":"Resident B is wearing a LifeVest and today signed a POLST selecting \"Do Not Attempt Resuscitation/DNR.\" What should the nurse do?","c":["Remove the LifeVest immediately because the resident is now DNR","Leave the LifeVest on and do nothing; the POLST does not apply to devices","Leave the LifeVest on, notify the attending provider/cardiologist right away to reconcile the device with the resident's wishes, document, and update the care plan","Tell the resident they cannot be DNR while wearing a LifeVest"],"a":2},{"q":"Which is correct when a resident wearing a LifeVest wants to shower?","c":["Remove the vest just before the shower, keep the shower brief with staff nearby, put the vest back on immediately afterward, confirm the monitor is on and monitoring, and document the time off and on","The vest can stay on in the shower because it is water resistant","Remove the vest in the morning and put it back on at bedtime","The resident may not shower while prescribed a LifeVest"],"a":0}]}},{"id":53,"key":"COC_Documentation_Reassessment","month":10,"title":"Change in Condition — Documentation and Medication Reassessment (F580/F658)","guidelines":["A change in condition is not finished when you give the medication. Every change follows the same loop: ASSESS → INTERVENE → REASSESS → DOCUMENT → ESCALATE if it did not work (NS-CIC-001 IX.A: implement orders and monitor the resident's response).","Reassess the same thing you treated, with the same measure you used before the dose: pain score with the same scale (or PAINAD), temperature, SpO₂ and respiratory rate, blood pressure and pulse, blood glucose, or the target behavior.","Reassess when the medication should be working: as the order specifies; acute pain every 30–60 minutes until relief (NS-PAIN-001); oxygen and PRN breathing treatments before and after each use (NS-O2-001); low blood sugar 15 minutes after treatment (NS-DM-001). As a general guide, oral medications 30–60 minutes and injected, inhaled, or sublingual medications 15–30 minutes.","Go back to the bedside. \"Resting quietly\" or \"no complaints\" is not a reassessment. Never chart \"effective\" without seeing the resident and repeating the measurement.","Document objectively, with times: the finding before the dose, the reason (indication) for the PRN, the drug, dose, route, and time, the finding after the dose, any side effects, and what you did next. \"Tolerated well\" and \"effective\" alone do not meet the standard (NS-DOC-001; MED-PASS Documentation of Medication Administration).","If the medication did not work, or caused a side effect, that is a change in condition. Notify the provider with an SBAR that includes the before and after values, follow the new orders, notify the representative, and document it (Title 22 §72311(a)(3): untoward response to medication or treatment). Do not repeat a dose or add a medication without an order.","Complete the INTERACT SBAR and the progress note before the end of your shift. Record every notification (who, date, time, method, response). A late entry is labeled \"late entry\" and shows both the time written and the time of the event, and it is never backdated. Update the care plan and tell the MDS Coordinator and DON about significant changes.","Move the resident to alert charting. Update the alert charting note at the end of EACH shift (current findings, reassessment results, response to interventions, new notifications or orders) until the resident is taken off alert. The oncoming nurse reads it at the start of the shift."],"roles":{"Licensed Nurse":[{"q":"T / F — After giving a PRN medication, the nurse reassesses the same symptom using the same measure used before the dose (for example, the same pain scale).","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — If the resident is resting quietly with eyes closed after a PRN pain medication, the nurse may chart the medication as effective without going back to reassess.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — \"Medication given, tolerated well\" is enough documentation for a PRN follow-up.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — A PRN medication that does not produce the intended effect is itself a change in condition, and the provider may need to be notified.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — A late entry may be dated and timed as if it had been written at the time of the event, so the chart reads in order.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — A resident with a change in condition is moved to alert charting, and the alert note is updated at the end of each shift until the resident is taken off alert.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — After an antipyretic is given for a temperature of 101.8°F, rechecking the temperature is optional if the resident says they feel better.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"At 0700 Resident A rates hip pain 8/10 on the 0–10 scale, and you give the ordered PRN oral opioid. What is the correct follow-up?","c":["Check the pain score at the end of the shift","Within 30–60 minutes, re-score the pain on the same 0–10 scale, check respiratory rate and level of sedation, see whether the resident can move or participate in care, and document the result in the PRN follow-up and the progress note","Wait until the next scheduled medication pass","Reassess only if the resident puts on the call light again"],"a":1},{"q":"Which progress note BEST meets the documentation standard?","c":["\"Resident appears uncomfortable. MD aware.\"","\"Neb given. Effective.\"","\"1415: Resident B short of breath, SpO₂ 86% on room air, RR 28, wheezing both lungs. 1420: PRN albuterol nebulizer given per order. 1445: SpO₂ 93% on room air, RR 20, wheezing decreased. 1450: NP notified by phone of episode and response; no new orders. 1500: daughter notified.\"","\"Resident had a bad afternoon but is better now.\""],"a":2},{"q":"At 1000, Resident C's blood pressure is 188/102 and you give the PRN antihypertensive per the ordered parameters. At 1045 the blood pressure is 184/100 and the resident reports a new headache. What should you do?","c":["Give a second dose of the PRN medication","Document \"ineffective\" and recheck on the next shift","Notify the provider now with an SBAR that includes the before and after blood pressures and the new headache, follow the orders received, notify the representative, and document all of it","Wait for the provider's next scheduled visit"],"a":2}]}},{"id":54,"key":"BG_Hyperglycemia","month":10,"title":"Blood Glucose Out of Range — Hyperglycemia Response (F684/F580)","guidelines":["Every resident who receives insulin or an oral diabetes medication must have resident-specific orders for blood-glucose (BG) monitoring, high and low practitioner notification parameters, and hypoglycemia treatment (NS-DM-001). If a resident with diabetes has no BG order or no parameters, call the practitioner to obtain them. Do not wait.","A BG outside the resident's ordered notification parameters is a change in condition. Assess the resident, notify the practitioner, follow the orders received, notify the resident representative of a significant change, and document all of it (NS-DM-001 IX; NS-CIC-001; F580).","If no parameters are ordered, notify the practitioner IMMEDIATELY for a meter reading of \"HI\" or a high BG with vomiting, inability to take fluids, rapid or deep breathing, fruity breath, lethargy, or confusion; and the SAME DAY for BG above 250 mg/dL more than once in 24 hours, BG above 300 mg/dL on two consecutive days, symptoms of high blood sugar, poor intake, or signs of infection (NS-DM-001 IX.1).","For a \"HI\" reading or an unexpected high value, wash the resident's hands and the site and recheck with a fresh fingerstick. Then assess vital signs, level of consciousness, hydration, intake and output, and signs of infection. Encourage fluids if not restricted.","Signs of DKA/HHS or a sudden decline: call 911 per the advance directive/POLST and notify the practitioner.","Give correctional or one-time insulin ONLY as specifically ordered. After treatment, recheck the BG when the order or practitioner directs and report a value that is not coming down. A high BG followed by another high BG the next morning is persistent hyperglycemia: notify the practitioner before giving the next scheduled diabetes medication.","If an ordered diabetes medication (such as insulin) is not available, notify the practitioner for direction and the pharmacy. Charting \"not available\" on the MAR is not enough.","A BG value on the MAR is not documentation of the event. For every glycemic event, the progress note shows: BG before treatment; symptoms and level of consciousness; each treatment (type, amount, route, time); each recheck BG and time; vital signs; practitioner notification (name, time, orders received); representative notification; and the outcome (NS-DM-001 XI.2). Complete the SBAR and note before the end of your shift, move the resident to alert charting, and update it at the end of each shift until the resident is off alert."],"roles":{"Licensed Nurse":[{"q":"T / F — A blood glucose outside the resident's ordered notification parameters is a change in condition that requires practitioner notification.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — If the BG value and the insulin given are both recorded on the MAR, the glycemic event is fully documented.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — When the glucometer reads \"HI,\" the nurse washes the resident's hands and the site, rechecks with a fresh fingerstick, and notifies the practitioner immediately.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — When the BG is very high, the nurse may give extra rapid-acting insulin based on nursing judgment without an order.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"T / F — If a resident with diabetes is admitted without a blood-glucose monitoring order or notification parameters, the nurse contacts the practitioner to obtain them.","c":["True","False"],"a":0},{"q":"T / F — When a scheduled insulin is not available from the pharmacy, charting \"not available\" on the MAR is sufficient.","c":["True","False"],"a":1},{"q":"A resident's BG order has no notification parameters. Which finding requires SAME-DAY practitioner notification under NS-DM-001?","c":["BG of 180 mg/dL once before lunch","BG above 250 mg/dL twice within 24 hours","BG of 140 mg/dL before breakfast","BG of 110 mg/dL at bedtime"],"a":1},{"q":"Which progress note BEST documents a hyperglycemia event?","c":["\"BS high, insulin given per order.\"","\"2105: BG 468 mg/dL. Resident alert, oriented to person, c/o thirst, no vomiting; BP 138/76, P 88, R 18, T 98.2. 2110: NP Lee notified by phone, one-time order insulin lispro 12 units SC received and given L abdomen. 2115: granddaughter notified by phone. 2240: BG recheck 301 mg/dL, NP Lee updated, no new orders. SBAR completed; placed on alert charting.\"","\"Resident's sugar was elevated this evening. MD aware.\"","\"See MAR.\""],"a":1},{"q":"At 2100 Resident D's BG is 480 mg/dL. You call the NP and receive a one-time order for rapid-acting insulin, and you give it. What else must happen before the end of your shift?","c":["Nothing; the insulin and the BG are on the MAR","Assess the resident, recheck the BG as the NP directs and report the result, notify the representative, complete the SBAR and a progress note, place the resident on alert charting, and hand off the recheck plan to the oncoming nurse","Recheck the BG at the next scheduled morning check","Ask the oncoming nurse to write the note"],"a":1},{"q":"The next morning at 0600, Resident D's BG is 405 mg/dL. Her oral diabetes medications are due at 0900. What do you do?","c":["Record the BG on the MAR and give the 0900 medications as scheduled","Wait for the practitioner's next visit","Assess the resident and notify the practitioner now with an SBAR that includes last night's value, the insulin given, and this morning's value; follow the orders received before giving the next diabetes medications; notify the representative; document and continue alert charting","Give a dose of insulin from the emergency kit"],"a":2}]}}];
